  @uncommitted_feature_work [
    "~560 lines of in-progress work predates the plan. COHERENT FEATURE SET, not scratch — see PLAN.md 'Uncommitted work in the tree'."
    "Resource economy: upload/analyze/probe/whois/nslookup converted to background processes with CPU/RAM/bandwidth costs."
    "Content redaction (contentRedaction.ts + 8 call sites) — SUPERSEDED by KNOWLEDGE_DESIGN.md."
    "Access-key discovery: scanForAccessKeys (cat hint) + detectAndGrantAccessKeys returning granted names (download)."
    "Tutorial alt-path: Training Firewall → accessMethod 'hack_or_key', seeded hidden .fw_maintenance.key on the Gateway, Architect mail teaches social engineering."
    "Lore de-naming: [AIDA] Primary Node → Unknown Signal Source, [AIDA] Archive → Phantom Archive, [AIDA] Mesh Router → Ghost Relay. Same 'earn it' theme as redaction."
    "BLOCKER (FIXED 2026-08-31, U1): the new tutorial mail instructs 'try ls -a' but ls -a WAS BROKEN (G8 — resolvePath consumed -a as the path → 'Directory not found: /-a'). Tokenizer landed; ls -a verified working end-to-end over the real socket path."
    "U1 VERIFIED 2026-08-31 by server/scripts/verify-tutorial-altpath.ts — 11 assertions, 11 PASS, and stable across repeated runs since the connect assertion was hardened (it used to read the command OUTPUT TEXT, which passed while the player was still on the previous server; it now POLLS for the ServerConnection row, because completeConnection writes that row AFTER handshake.ack emits its result). Drives register → authenticate → topology hops (auto-solving TCP handshake challenges) → ls -a → cat → download → connect. Re-run this after ANY change to tutorial steps, access keys, or connect/adjacency."
    "U1 FINDING 1 (fixed): tutorial step 5 was a HARD WALL — completable by NEITHER advertised route. Objective was {type:'hack'}, and hack objectives are credited ONLY by hackService.onHackComplete, so the key route granted access then left the step permanently incomplete. The hack route is gated at Hacking 20 while players arrive with 10. Fixed by new 'breach_server' objective type credited by BOTH onHackComplete and a new missionIntegration.onAccessGranted() hook fired from networkCommands.completeConnection() (the single funnel for direct + post-challenge connects, so it covers key AND backdoor entry)."
    "U1 DESIGN RULE: onAccessGranted deliberately does NOT credit the hack* family (hack/hack_target/hack_stealth/hack_method). Those name the ACT of hacking — crediting them from a found key would let any 'hack N servers' mission be completed by looting credentials. The harness asserts both directions."
    "U1 FINDING 2 (near-miss, caught before landing): gain_access was the obvious fix and would have been a REGRESSION. It requires accessLevel >= minLevel (default 1), but a scraped 'minimal' breach reports success:true with accessLevel = floor(x * 0.1) = 0 (hackService.ts:1050). Routing the tutorial through gain_access would have broken the hack path for exactly the low-skill players the tutorial exists for. Hence a dedicated breach_server type. Latent wart left alone: `metadata?.minLevel || 1` makes minLevel:0 unrepresentable."
    "U1 FINDING 3 (fixed): the tutorial's FIRST instruction did not work. connect requires a DIRECT link (canTraverse), and a new player's home terminal links only to Internet Exchange (10.0.0.1) — yet step 1's hint said 'connect 10.10.10.1' → CONNECTION BLOCKED. Steps 1/2/5 now teach the real route 10.0.0.1 → 10.10.10.1 → …. Step 5's mail also led with brute force and recommended 'backdoor install' (Hacking 50, unreachable); it now leads with credentials, states the Hacking 20 requirement, and drops the backdoor advice."
    "U1 CHECKED AND FINE: the first-visit connection challenge IS taught — a one-time Architect briefing mail fires on the first challenge (connectionChallengeService) and is DB-checked so it survives restart. Also: first visit to ANY non-home server always challenges, regardless of accessMethod/isPublic."
    "VERIFIED SOUND: hack_or_key IS handled in networkTopologyService.ts:741 and networkCommands.ts:416; seeded key + hidden file are created correctly."
    "FIXED (S11) — verified 2026-09-24. checkServerAccess's switch no longer ends in a permissive default: an unrecognized accessMethod now FAILS CLOSED (networkTopologyService.ts:763-786), and the socket-path wrapper fails closed too (gameStateManager.ts:566-570, 595-602). The create_server agent tool can still write an arbitrary accessMethod string, but an unknown value now denies access instead of granting it."
  ]

  @navigation_cd_ls [
    "Full detail in SHELL_DESIGN.md §10a (N1-N10). Fixes ride along with the Phase 1 tokenizer (decision 7)."
    "DONE 2026-08-30: shared/shell/ created (lexer.ts + parser.ts + args.ts + index.ts). Wired into commandProcessor via tryShellParse(), which FALLS BACK to the legacy whitespace split on non-simple ASTs (pipe/redirect/&&/;) and on ShellParseError (apostrophes in free text). No existing input can regress."
    "DONE: N1 (ls -l / -a / -la now work — flags separated from positionals in ls/rm/cp/mv), N3+N4 (~ has ONE meaning: home dir on your own home server; refuses elsewhere with 'no home directory on this host'), N5 (bare cd goes home not /), N6 (cd silent on success), N9 (cd -, backed by new PlayerSession.previousDirectory)."
    "DONE 2026-08-30 N2: FileService.statPath() added — existence + type + readability in O(path depth), reusing resolvePath's already-loaded node (zero extra queries on the common path). cd uses it instead of listDirectory and now rejects `cd <file>`. ALSO fixed the underlying N+1: listDirectory passed child.id to canRead, which re-fetches when given a string — every ls paid one extra findUnique per entry. Now passes the loaded row."
    "DONE 2026-08-30 N7: columns() added to asciiBox.ts — width-aware column-major packing (reading DOWN a column is alphabetical, like real ls). Bare ls is dense text; ls -l stays boxed (it IS a report). Markers use ls -F convention: / dir, * encrypted, + protected, with a legend only when present. Measured 24 entries: ~28 lines → 6 at width 70."
    "STILL OPEN: N8 (no tree, no find), N10 (ls -l falls back to literal 'rwxr-xr-x')."
    "VERIFIED 2026-08-31 (U1): ls -a reveals hidden entries and bare ls hides them, tested with a positive control (the non-hidden sibling must still be listed) so an empty result cannot pass vacuously. columns() output confirmed dense."
    "SUPERSEDED — the six pre-fix navigation descriptions that used to sit here (cd's per-file N+1, one-entry-per-line ls, `ls -l`/`ls -a` failing outright, cd ~ resolving to a cuid, three conflicting meanings of ~, and bare cd going to /) are ALL closed by the DONE entries above, which were added directly on top of them without deleting them — the section header's rule, 'delete each entry as it is fixed', had not been followed. Current truth: N1 (flags separated from positionals), N2 (statPath, which also killed the ls N+1), N3+N4 (~ has ONE meaning), N5, N6, N7 (columns()) and N9 (cd -) are all DONE. Only N8 (no tree, no find) and N10 (ls -l falls back to a literal 'rwxr-xr-x') remain open — both stated in the STILL OPEN entry above."
    "NO tree and NO find commands exist — verified zero matches repo-wide. In a game about exploring filesystems, structure below the current level is invisible."
    "PRESENTATION RULE: boxes for things read once (whois/probe/briefings); dense text for things read constantly (ls/cd/pwd). Keep ls -l boxed — that one IS a report."
    "GOOD: the prompt is already username@server:cwd$ (Terminal.svelte:1479). Orientation is solved — do not change it."
  ]
}

@phase1_gate_closed {
  @result [
    "PHASE 1 GATE CLOSED 2026-08-31. All five legs verified by execution: scripts/verify-gate-phase1.ts 15/15 (mission accept->complete, shop buy->inventory, encryption gate) plus scripts/verify-tutorial-altpath.ts 11/11."
  ]

  @three_bugs_the_harness_found [
    "1. THE MISSION ECONOMY HAD NEVER STARTED. missionCommands auto-generated only `if (missions.length === 0)`, but the tutorial auto-assigns a mission on first login so that count is never 0 for a real player. generateMissionsForPlayer NEVER RAN ONCE — all 9 missions in the DB were tutorial missions and no player had ever been offered a generated one."
    "2. EVEN WITH OFFERS, NONE COULD BE ACCEPTED. acceptMission required the mission to already be in the player's mission state ('Mission not assigned to player'), but a generated mission is an offer in the Mission table with assignedTo:null. THIS SAT BENEATH THE G2 STATUS FIX — G2 was necessary but insufficient. !!! THE FIX DESCRIBED HERE WAS REVERTED 2026-08-31 — DO NOT REAPPLY IT !!! This entry used to end 'accepting an open offer now materialises the progress entry and claims the row so two players cannot take the same offer.' missionService.ts:545-559 records that change as reverted: it was unnecessary (missionGenerator.ts:186-215 already writes the offer into the player's own state via mutateAll) AND harmful (it let two players hold the same mission). CURRENT TRUTH: offers are PER-PLAYER, never an anonymous claimable pool; acceptMission hard-requires the mission to be on offer to YOU (missionService.ts:580-582). Re-implementing the 'claim the row' behaviour reintroduces the bug."
    "3. GENERATION BLOCKED THE COMMAND. generateMissionsForPlayer is AI-bound and the AI service was rate-limiting with 5s/15s backoff, so the first player to empty the offer pool got NO RESPONSE AT ALL (measured >180s, nothing rendered). Now fired in the background, panel returns immediately saying contracts are being drafted. Same failure shape and same remedy as inline persona-reply generation."
  ]

  @i_broke_it_in_between [
    "My first fix for (1) filtered the PLAYER'S OWN missions for 'available' — but offers deliberately are not there, so the count was always 0 and `missions` regenerated on EVERY call, hanging outright. WORSE than the bug it replaced. The guard now counts unclaimed Mission rows, where offers actually live."
    "Verified in BOTH directions: pool non-empty -> responds 0.3s and does NOT regenerate; pool empty -> responds 0.3s and offers appear in background. A one-directional test would have passed the broken version."
  ]

  @my_own_assertions_were_wrong_twice [
    "canAccessServer takes a server ID, not an IP. Passing IPs made every G4 row return 'Server not found', which READS AS A REFUSAL — so 'gate refuses under-level player' passed for entirely the wrong reason. The positive control ('unencrypted server is NOT gated') is what exposed it."
    "The generator-guard probe sampled the offer pool ONCE, immediately after a call I had just made asynchronous, and reported two false failures. Tests must keep up with the design they test."
    "Method learnings §2 (every negative assertion needs a positive control) earned its place twice in one sitting."
  ]
}

# !!! DUPLICATE TOP-LEVEL KEY !!!
# This is the SECOND @mission_system block; the first is at line 788. A TOON reader
# that builds a map will have one silently shadow the other. They DISAGREE on seven
# points; audited 2026-09-24, source verdicts:
#   1. bonus objectives    -> BLOCK A (788) is right; this block is pre-R7
#   2. timeLimit 1000x     -> BOTH STALE; fixed, seconds are canonical
#   3. prerequisites       -> THIS BLOCK is right: never enforced, zero readers
#   4. completeMission     -> NEITHER: claim-then-pay under a mutex, no $transaction;
#                             the re-entrant reward loop is CLOSED (index.ts:183-191)
#   5. `tutorial` command  -> BLOCK A is wrong; no such command exists server-side
#   6. generation pipeline -> NOT in conflict: A documents the persona path
#                             (personaMissionGenService), this block the template path
#   7. counts              -> NEITHER: 37 objective types, 15 integration hooks
# Use BLOCK A (788) as the structural reference; use THIS block for @dual_state,
# @root_causes and @dead_code. M15 below is the one still-open argument-level bug.
@mission_system {
  @read_first [
    "FULL ARCHITECTURE: MISSIONS_ARCHITECTURE.md (2026-08-31). Read it before touching missions. Written after the maintainer stopped three consecutive symptom-patches, two of which were WRONG because I had no model of the system."
  ]

  @dual_state [
    "State lives in TWO places: the Mission table (definition + a single status/assignedTo) and PlayerProgress.missionProgress JSON (per-player status, objectives, timestamps). Objectives exist in BOTH: the table copy is a template, the blob copy is the only one ever advanced."
    "getPlayerMissions() reads ONLY the blob. getAvailableMissions(), tutorial position, token gating and admin routes read ONLY the table. This asymmetry governs everything."
    "OFFERS ARE PER PLAYER. missionGenerator writes each generated mission into that player's own blob as 'available' (missionGenerator.ts:178-205) specifically so getPlayerMissions returns it. DO NOT treat offers as an anonymous global pool — it breaks per-player level scaling and lets two players hold and complete the same mission."
  ]

  @root_causes [
    "M1 FIXED: the auto-generate guard was `missions.length === 0`, unreachable because the tutorial pre-assigns a mission on first login. generateMissionsForPlayer had NEVER RUN — every mission in the DB was a tutorial mission."
    "M2 OPEN (the reason offers still don't reach players): createMission -> provisionMissionInfrastructure creates a server via an AI-bound, RATE-LIMITED path, per mission, and the blob write-back sits at the END of the loop, all-or-nothing. Generation takes minutes and typically never reaches the write-back. Mitigated by no longer awaiting generation inline; the write-back needs to be incremental, or provisioning deferred to accept-time."
  ]

  @unwinnable_missions [
    "~15 OF 39 TEMPLATES ARE IMPOSSIBLE TO COMPLETE, from four argument-level bugs — not from missing handlers. Every objective type HAS a crediting branch."
    "M11: hackService.ts:1435 passes the target USER id into a parameter that is compared against metadata.serverId. hack_target unwinnable in EVERY case (7 templates). targetServerId is in scope and unused. install_backdoor/breach_server survive only by accident, being unbound so matchesEntity returns true — i.e. credited by hacking ANY server."
    "M12: both download call sites (fileCommands.ts:226, :330) pass fileId: \"\". `steal` unwinnable whenever provisioning succeeded (8 templates); `exfiltrate_data` unwinnable unconditionally. Cruel detail: getObjectiveHint('steal') tells the player to use `read`, which never credits it."
    "M13: serverContentService's provisioning allow-list omits infiltrate_network/trace_connection/exfiltrate_data/breach_server/install_backdoor/download_file/decode_content — even though the patch switch HAS case arms for three of them. deep_extraction consists only of omitted types => no server, no file, no metadata => fully unwinnable."
    "M14: boolean-credited objectives authored with target: 2 (kingmaker install_backdoor, intelligence_sweep decode_content). validateObjective CATCHES this but missionGenerator only LOGS A WARNING and creates it anyway."
    "M15: personaMissionGenService never provisions at all, and its fill-if-missing guard treats the literal '{targetServerId}' as already-filled, so placeholders are stored and compared against real ids."
  ]

  @rewards_lie [
    "skillPoints is shown in the reward toast and NEVER WRITTEN (13 templates promise 1-5). unlocks NEVER granted (aida_trail promises aida_knowledge, endgame promises endgame_access). reputation ALWAYS credits repNeutral regardless of mission.factionId."
    "bonusObjectivesCompleted = max(0, completed - total) is structurally always 0, so the bonus multiplier is dead code."
    "grantRewards emits rewards:xp_granted / rewards:credits_granted which RE-ENTER updateObjective -> completeMission -> grantRewards. Re-entrant loop bounded only by objectives running out of headroom."
  ]

  @dead_code [
    "assignMission() is the only writer of status 'assigned' and has ZERO callers. checkAllActiveMissions() has ZERO callers and is the only path to expireMission. cleanupExpiredMissions() has ZERO callers. MISSION_CATEGORIES has zero consumers. Prerequisites are declared and never enforced."
    "Status vocabulary is broken three ways: 'active' is the dominant value (118 uses) and is NOT in the shared enum (shipped to the client via an unchecked cast); 'in_progress' is declared and never written; 'abandoned' is written and never declared."
    "timeLimit is wrong by 1000x: templates declare seconds, the generator converts to ms, then expiresAt = now + timeLimit*1000 treats ms as seconds. Generated missions expire in ~41-83 days and the time bonus always pays."
  ]

  @fix_order [
    "REVISED: argument bugs FIRST (M11-M14) — hours of work, each resurrects whole groups of missions. Then honesty/payout (M16 skillPoints+unlocks, M17 reputation, M3 bonus-objective completion gate, M4 timeLimit units). Then M2 (the generated-mission root cause). Then the structural dual-store cleanup with Phase 3's PlayerProgressRepository."
    "VERIFY M11-M14 against a DIRECTLY SEEDED mission — until M2 lands, offers never reach players, so none of the crediting defects is observable in play."
  ]

  @verified_working [
    "Tutorial path 11/11. Accept -> active -> objective completion -> stickiness 15/15. REAL GAMEPLAY DOES credit objectives: driven with actual `connect` calls, 'Connect to 3 different servers' advanced 0 -> 1 -> 3 and completed at target."
    "NOT verified: no generated or persona mission has ever been completed by anyone. §11 of the doc is static analysis, not measurement."
  ]
}

@method_learnings {
  @rules [
    "1. EACH FIX REVEALS THE NEXT LAYER. Five instances: G1 masked by G2; S2 by G3; G3's reward path by inventory filtering; the tutorial key route by objective crediting; `hack` itself by ownerless servers. The first fix was correct every time and the feature still did not work. RULE: after fixing something, run the whole player-visible flow, not the unit you touched."
    "2. VERIFY BY EXECUTION, AND GIVE EVERY NEGATIVE ASSERTION A POSITIVE CONTROL. Static reading missed all three U1 defects and the ownerless-server P0 entirely. My own harnesses produced VACUOUS PASSES twice — 'hidden file absent' against empty output, and 'fallback delivered' because the AI answered a junk prompt. A negative assertion without a positive control is not a test."
    "3. getService<any> ERASES CONTRACTS — 53 REMAIN (vs 46 typed, measured 2026-08-31). The onFactionServerHacked arity bug survived because its call site was untyped while the correct one two functions away was typed. Typing the call sites IS the arity-bug fix; hunting mismatches by inspection cannot scale to 53 blind spots."
    "4. FIX THE BUG CLASS, NOT THE INSTANCE. I removed one `as any` and reintroduced the identical pattern in networkCommands in the SAME session, having just written the lesson down. An audit caught it, not me. Grep for the pattern before closing an item."
    "5. INVISIBLE FALLBACKS ARE THE REAL AI PROBLEM, AND IT IS NOW MEASURABLE. An NPC reaction fell back and the entire run emitted ONE log line (mine); safeAI's failure path logged nothing even at debug. npcReactionService's explicit `usedAi` flag is the pattern to generalise across 41+ safeAI sites."
    "6. DOCUMENTATION ROTS NUMERICALLY, AND THIS FILE NEEDS AUDITING TOO. PLAN.md audited clean (17/17) while THIS file claimed 61 models vs 66, listed 30 nonexistent migrations, omitted 5 models, and lacked `npc` on User.role. Prefer symbol names to line numbers; re-derive counts rather than copying them forward."
    "7. TUNING AGAINST A BROKEN SUBSYSTEM IS THE MOST EXPENSIVE MISTAKE AVAILABLE. U2's premise was wrong; G4's fix would have locked players out of their own homes; the hack gate was calibrated against a command that could not reach 44 of 59 servers. Verify the mechanism is LIVE before tuning its numbers, and prefer measuring the current effect over reasoning about it."
    "8. PREFER REMOVING A BUG CLASS TO MITIGATING IT. Phase 0 mitigated the shared/types.js shadowing by pinning Vite's resolve.extensions; deleting the redundant barrel removed the possibility entirely. Same shape as the soft gates: the hard gate was redundant with a system that already degraded gracefully."
  ]
}

@skill_economy {
  @decision [
    "MAINTAINER DECISION 2026-08-31: TWO award channels. Missions grant DEFINED skill points (e.g. +10 hacking); levelling up grants FREE points the player assigns freely."
    "Full spec in PLAN.md Phase 8 -> 'SKILL ECONOMY'. R8 (the missing PlayerProgress.skillPoints column) is only the storage half."
  ]

  @current_state_verified [
    "PlayerProgress.skillPoints is NOT in schema.prisma. 28 code sites mention skillPoints."
    "Level-up IS detected (missionService.ts:1106-1126) but GRANTS NOTHING — sets level, emits an event and a 'Level Up!' toast, nothing else."
    "NOTHING anywhere spends skill points. Zero call sites. `skills` command is view-only."
    "MissionReward.skillPoints is a BARE NUMBER with no skill named, so it cannot express '+10 hacking'. 8 mission templates set it (1..5); dungeon intel_package sets 2."
    "Skills are Int 0-100. hackService already clamps with Math.min(gain, 100 - progress.hacking) — reuse, do not invent a second rule."
    "R8 severity, verified precisely: the ONLY Prisma write with skillPoints is darknetDungeonService.ts:1147-1149 (intel_package), spread into the SAME data object as experience increment — so Prisma rejects the unknown column, the whole update throws, safeExecute swallows it, and the XP is lost too. Fails 100% of the time it fires because the pool hardcodes skillPoints:2 at :122. Weight 4 of 22 => ~18% of dungeon conquests currently grant NOTHING. Mission rewards are NOT affected — they only display SP, never write it."
  ]

  @award_magnitudes [
    "MAINTAINER REFINEMENT 2026-08-31: award 1-2 points for mission completion AND successful hack attempts, scaled by RELATIVE difficulty (gap between player skill and attempt difficulty). The earlier '+10' was only an example."
    "LIVE BUG FOUND: hackService.ts:1371 calls awardExperience(attackerId, successRate, ...) but the parameter is NAMED `difficulty`. successRate is clamped 0.05-0.95, so ceil(difficulty*2) yields +1 for a HARD hack and +2 for an EASY one — the curve is EXACTLY INVERTED. Stealth (*1.5) has the same inversion. Both values are `number` so the compiler cannot see it: the arity-bug family in its most invisible form."
    "SILVER LINING: hacks already award 1-2 points — the requested magnitude, reached BY ACCIDENT. And successRate is ALREADY skill-relative (calculateHackParameters folds in player hacking, target forensics, server security, tools, encryption), so the fix is to correct its POLARITY and add a zero band, NOT to build a new difficulty-minus-skill calculation."
    "PROPOSED BANDS (gameBalance.ts): successRate >= 0.85 -> 0 points (trivial for you); 0.50-0.85 -> 1; < 0.50 -> 2. Cap the final award at 2 AFTER xpMultiplier, since a full breach multiplies by 1.5 and would yield 3. Self-limiting: as skill rises, successRate on a fixed target rises, so awards decay to 0 with no extra bookkeeping."
    "THE ZERO BAND IS NOT OPTIONAL: at 1 point per successful hack with no floor, ~90 farmed hacks on the Training Firewall max a skill."
    "OPEN DECISION — FAILURES: failure currently awards a flat +1 hacking and that is LOAD-BEARING (it is the learn-by-failing loop U3's soft gates depend on). Zeroing it reintroduces the chicken-and-egg. Suggested: same relative rule, so failing a HARD target still teaches while failing an easy one does not. Missions granting defined points now provide an independent early-game path, so failure rewards need not carry that alone."
    "MISSIONS have no successRate and need their own normalization: mission difficulty is 1-10 while skills are 0-100. Simplest consistent rule: compare difficulty*10 against the player's level in the NAMED skill, same three bands."
  ]

  @balance_flag [
    "SUPERSEDED by @award_magnitudes: +10 was an example, actual awards are 1-2. At 1-2 per mission/hack, reaching `hack`'s baseline of 20 from the starting 10 takes ~5-10 awards rather than one, so the soft-gate penalty curve STAYS load-bearing — which is the better outcome. Still decide magnitudes alongside U2b/U3c so the early-game curve is tuned ONCE."
  ]
}

@audit_2026_08_31 {
  @migrations_deferred [
    "DECISION 2026-08-31 (maintainer): defer migrations until there is a live DB. I had just rebuilt them into a clean 0001_init; the maintainer's call to drop them instead is better, and the reasoning is worth keeping — a single baseline is only 'create all 66 tables', which db push already does from schema.prisma. Migrations pay off when evolving a database you cannot drop, and none exists pre-launch."
    "prisma/migrations/ deleted AND the _prisma_migrations table dropped, so the DB is in a pure db-push state with no phantom history to inherit at adoption time. db:deploy REMOVED (migrate deploy cannot work without migrations — leaving it would be a trap). db:reset re-pointed at `db push --force-reset && seed`."
    "Corrected my own Phase 0 D1 justification: it claimed the blanket *.sql rule meant a fresh clone 'could not provision a database at all'. FALSE — it always could, from schema.prisma. Ignoring migrations costs DEPLOY HISTORY, not provisioning."
    "HONEST GAP: the destructive db:reset path is NOT end-to-end verified — the run was blocked by a safety check. --force-reset is confirmed present in prisma 5.22.0 and both halves are individually known-good, but it needs one manual run before being trusted."
  ]

  @source_of_truth_correction [
    "MAINTAINER CORRECTION 2026-08-31: I told the maintainer 'the migrations directory is now the source of truth for schema'. That is BACKWARDS. schema.prisma is the declarative authority and is tracked in git; 0001_init was GENERATED from it via `migrate diff`, so migrations are a derived artifact reproducible in one command. Losing prisma/migrations/ is therefore recoverable, not catastrophic — it should still be committed as deployment history, but it is not the authority."
    "The rule the maintainer set: schema knowledge must live in either schema.prisma or PROJECT_KNOWLEDGE — never only in a derived migration SQL file. @database_schema and @migrations now both state this explicitly."
    "This exposed that PROJECT_KNOWLEDGE's own data-model docs had drifted (my audit had only checked PLAN.md against code, not the knowledge file). Fixed: models 61 -> 66; five undocumented models added (ContentDraft, ContentJob, PendingPersonaMail, EpochEvent, MessageReport) in new @ai_pipeline_and_queues and @moderation groups; User.role gained `npc`, which had been missing even though npc-role accounts now own every NPC server; seed.ts 2833 -> 3286 lines; services 52 -> 62, DI tokens 55 -> 58, registrations 52 -> 55."
    "@migrations was the worst case: it listed 30 migrations by name that NO LONGER EXIST on disk, and had already been stale before this session (the schema was being maintained with `db push` while a 1798-line 0001_baseline sat unapplied). Replaced with the current single baseline, the regeneration recipe, and a note on why the phantom list was deleted rather than preserved — documenting migrations that cannot be applied is worse than documenting none."
  ]

  @summary [
    "PLAN.md re-checked against the code by two independent read-only passes. ALL 17 Phase 0 / G-item assertions CONFIRMED — no WRONG, no STALE. The U-items produced 6 corrections, all applied."
    "BIGGEST FINDING (process, not code): NOTHING IS COMMITTED. 26 modified + 18 untracked paths, including 4 new source files and the entire prisma/migrations/ directory. They are NOT gitignored (so they CAN be committed) but remain uncommitted. My PLAN wording 'tracked, unlike scripts/' was an overstatement — corrected to 'committable'."
    "Stale doc claims fixed: the 0002_pending_persona_mail migration no longer exists (rebuilt as single 0001_init after the authorised reset); U3d-follow's email-domain cap predicate was DELETED by U3d-follow-2 not rewired; 3 line citations had drifted because later work edited the same files; matchesEntity is at 9 sites not the 7 I claimed; 6 superseded -orig entries were still unchecked checkboxes inflating the open backlog."
  ]

  @code_defects_found [
    "networkCommands.ts called onAccessGranted through `(missionIntegration as any)` — the EXACT untyped-call pattern whose removal U3d credits for exposing the onFactionServerHacked arity bug. I fixed one instance and introduced another in the same session. Now typed. LESSON: when you fix a bug class, grep for the pattern instead of fixing only the instance you found."
    "breach_server's trackedBy metadata named only onHackComplete, under-reporting the onAccessGranted route that is the type's whole reason to exist. Fixed."
    "AI_IDENTITY_EMAIL_DOMAINS + isSyntheticAiEmail were dead code — added for the cap fix, orphaned when the cap was removed. Deleted."
  ]

  @latent_hazards_closed [
    "shared/types.ts (a 5-line barrel) coexisted with shared/types/index.ts, so `shared/types` resolved AMBIGUOUSLY and extension order stayed load-bearing — the same mechanism behind the ReservedPID misdiagnosis, which Phase 0 mitigated only by pinning Vite's resolve.extensions. The barrel is now DELETED; all 61 imports resolve to the directory index. Verified: server tsc clean, svelte-check 0, vite build succeeds. This removes the bug class rather than mitigating it."
    "A local `const columns` in systemCommands.ts shadowed the imported columns() helper from asciiBox. Block scoping made it harmless BY ACCIDENT; renamed to tableColumns."
  ]

  @db_reset [
    "2026-08-31: maintainer authorised a reset, so migrations were rebuilt properly (D2, pulled forward from Phase 3). Single 0001_init generated via `migrate diff --from-empty --to-schema-datamodel` (1902 lines, 66 tables), applied by `migrate reset --force` which also ran the seed. `migrate status` now says 'Database schema is up to date!'. Added migration_lock.toml + db:deploy / db:reset scripts."
    "Post-reset verified: 38 servers 0 ownerless, 5 NPC display-named accounts with persona emails and defender profiles, pending_persona_mail reachable, 19 shop items, and U1 11/11 FROM SCRATCH — which validates seed + migrations + all Phase 1/U work end to end."
  ]

  @replies_outside_budget [
    "MAINTAINER REQUIREMENT 2026-08-31: replies must work outside AI_MAX_ACTIONS_PER_DAY. Verified they already did (canTakeAction is consulted ONLY in aiSchedulerService.processScheduledAction; a reply delivered with actionsToday=13 vs cap 3 without incrementing it), but the exemption was IMPLICIT. Now documented at both ends as an invariant: the daily budget covers AUTONOMOUS activity only."
    "That exposed a hole: the flood limit caps DELIVERY while generation happens before it, so spamming `msg` could queue unbounded AI calls. Replies now COALESCE — one pending reply per sender->player, refreshed to the newest question but keeping the original deliverAt so nagging cannot move the answer. Verified: 12 messages -> 1 pending generation, latest question wins, notice-kind mail not swallowed."
  ]
}

@persona_mail {
  @cap_removed [
    "DONE 2026-08-31: the global '20 AI messages/day' cap in sendAIMessage is REMOVED. It was the wrong control three ways: (1) it could NOT save AI cost — `content` arrives already generated, so tokens were spent before the check ran; (2) most of what it blocked was not AI output at all — tutorial architectMail and CONNECTION_BRIEFING_CONTENT are static string constants; (3) being GLOBAL it let one persona send a single player 20 messages while stopping 20 players from getting one each — backwards from inbox protection."
    "The legitimate concern is already handled in the right place: aiSchedulerService's per-persona AI_MAX_ACTIONS_PER_DAY (default 3, env-configurable), checked in canTakeAction() BEFORE generation. NOTE it only covers SCHEDULED actions — event-driven sends (personaService, darknetDiscoveryService, architectInterventionExecutor) are not budgeted by it."
    "REPLACED BY per-recipient+per-sender flood protection: AI_MESSAGE_FLOOD_LIMIT=5 per AI_MESSAGE_FLOOD_WINDOW_MS=1h. Verified in all three dimensions — the limit bites, a different persona still reaches the same player, and an unrelated player is unaffected."
    "CORRECTION to an earlier claim in this session: tutorial step mail ALWAYS fell back to sendSystemMessage, so onboarding was degraded (lost the Architect's voice), NOT lost. The real casualty was connectionChallengeService, which IGNORED sendAIMessage's return value — so the briefing explaining the MANDATORY first-visit challenge vanished silently. Now falls back."
  ]

  @deferred_replies [
    "MAINTAINER'S INSIGHT (2026-08-31): AI mail replies should not be instant — it breaks immersion — and that delay can double as AI load management. Implemented as personaMailQueueService + the PendingPersonaMail table."
    "ONE MECHANISM, TWO PROBLEMS: (a) a persona answering the same second reads as a vending machine; (b) replies used to generate INLINE and synchronously, so a player's `msg` blocked on the model and bursts contended for the AI service's 2 concurrency slots."
    "Delay profiles with jitter: reply 45-210s, notice 15-75s. Jitter so simultaneous players don't get answers in lockstep."
    "KEY IDEA — BACKPRESSURE BECOMES CHARACTERISATION: new aiService.getLoad() exposes throttle saturation; when it is >= 0.5 the worker SLIDES deliverAt later instead of forcing a call or dropping the reply. 'Still composing' is in-fiction plausible, so the queue never needs to skip work. Verified by stubbing saturation to 0.95: the item deferred (not sent, not lost) and delivered on the next tick once load cleared."
    "Generation happens WHEN DUE, not at enqueue — that is what spreads a burst across time. enqueue() is a single INSERT, so the player's command returns immediately."
    "Failed generation retries on later ticks with growing backoff; once maxAttempts is spent the hand-written fallback is delivered, so a reply ALWAYS eventually lands. State is in the DB, so undelivered mail survives a restart."
    "Removed the '[AI response unavailable — static hint provided]' suffix from the tutorial fallback — it leaked implementation detail into the fiction."
  ]

  @side_fix [
    "sendPrivateMessage incremented messagesSent with `update`, which throws P2025 when the sender has no PlayerProgress — i.e. EVERY persona. The `.catch(() => {})` hid it in JS but PRISMA STILL LOGGED AN ERROR for every persona mail delivered, which looked like a real fault. Now `updateMany`, which matches zero rows silently. Boot is back to 0 error lines."
  ]

  @schema_note [
    "PendingPersonaMail was created via `prisma db execute` with a checked-in migration file (prisma/migrations/0002_pending_persona_mail), NOT `migrate dev`. Reason: the 0001 baseline is still UNAPPLIED because the schema was built with `db push`, so `migrate dev` would have offered to RESET the dev database. Reconciling migration state is Phase 3."
  ]
}

@npc_display_names {
  @done [
    "DONE 2026-08-31 (U3d-follow-3): ONE ACCOUNT PER CHARACTER, named for a player's eyes. NPC owners renamed npc_steele -> 'Commander Steele', npc_gh0st -> 'gh0st', npc_chen -> 'Director Chen', npc_aida -> 'AIDA', npc_sysadmin -> 'sysadmin'."
    "IT WAS A MERGE, NOT A RENAME: 'Commander Steele' already existed as a separate persona account, so renaming would collide on the unique username. Direction settled by DATA, not naming — the npc_* rows held everything (server ownership, 535 authored files on Steele alone, forum memberships) while the persona rows had ZERO references. So the server-owning row wins and adopts both the display name AND the persona's canonical email, which makes resolveAiPersonaUserId resolve to it BY EMAIL and stop minting a second account per character."
    "reconcileIdentity() in npcOwnership.ts: gathers every candidate row (legacy username, display name, persona email), picks the one owning the most servers as canonical, moves server ownership + sent messages off duplicates, handles forumMember's unique (userId,forumId) by dropping colliding rows rather than blind-moving, frees the duplicate's unique columns BEFORE the canonical claims them, then deletes it. Ordered so it never trips a unique constraint mid-flight."
    "Verified: 'renamed 5, merged 4' on first run, then COMPLETELY SILENT on re-run (idempotent). Owner == messenger for all 4 faction characters. Server counts preserved: AIDA 14, Steele 8, Chen 10, gh0st 6, sysadmin 6. Intrusion warnings now arrive from 'Commander Steele' / 'AIDA' / 'sysadmin'."
    "SEED updated to create these with display names directly, so a fresh DB is correct by construction rather than depending on the migration."
  ]

  @gotcha [
    "PRISMA TRAP (found via a false-positive in my own verification): `startsWith: \"npc_\"` compiles to SQL LIKE 'npc_%', and `_` is a SINGLE-CHARACTER WILDCARD — so it also matched the probe account 'npchack...'. It OVER-MATCHES silently rather than erroring. Repo-wide check found no remaining instances; the only one was sendAIMessage's `startsWith: \"ai_\"` cap query, already replaced with email-domain matching. Watch for this whenever a prefix ends in an underscore."
  ]
}

@ai_user_identity {
  @the_bug [
    "FIXED 2026-08-31. Four sites open-coded 'get a User account for this AI' with FOUR different answers for the @unique homeIp column: messageService.getAIUserId used '127.0.0.1' (BROKEN — only the first persona could ever be created, every one after failed the constraint); forumService post path used ipService.generateUniqueIP() (worked, but allocated from the PLAYER range); forumService reply path used '127.0.0.1' (same break); forumService npc-handle path used 127.0.<rand>.<rand> (collision merely unlikely, no retry)."
    "USER-FACING SYMPTOM: sendAIMessage worked for at most ONE persona. Verified fixed — all 5 personas now deliver."
  ]

  @solution [
    "src/utils/aiUserIdentity.ts replaces all four. resolveAiPersonaUserId(personaId, displayName?) and resolveNpcHandleUserId(handle)."
    "Addresses come from a RESERVED 0.0.x.y block — non-routable, visibly not a game address, extending the convention npcOwnership.ts already uses at 0.0.0.1-0.0.0.5. Slots 0-15 skipped (0.0.0.0 is SYSTEM, 1-5 are NPC owners)."
    "Allocation is DETERMINISTIC (sha256 of the key) then LINEAR-PROBED — a persona keeps the same address across runs, but a collision resolves instead of throwing."
    "Lookup order keys on EMAIL (derived from persona id, stable) BEFORE username (a display name that can be edited); legacy ai_<personaId> / npc_forum_<handle> id forms honoured first so existing rows keep resolving. Create is wrapped to re-read on a lost race rather than surfacing a constraint error."
  ]

  @adjacent_fixes [
    "sendAIMessage's daily-cap query counted only senders whose id startsWith 'ai_', so any persona account seeded with a normal cuid was invisible to the cap. Now matched on the AI email domains (AI_IDENTITY_EMAIL_DOMAINS)."
    "Legacy AI accounts created via the generateUniqueIP() path got role:'player' — so an AI PERSONA WAS COUNTED AS A PLAYER by adminApi/stats.ts, with a player-looking home IP. The resolver now normalizes any account it resolves to role:'npc'. Confirmed on the real 'The Architect' row, which was 'player'."
  ]

  @open [
    "U3d-follow-2: the AI message cap is 20/day GLOBAL across all personas, and TUTORIAL MAIL runs through it — each registration sends 'Your Training Begins' plus the connection-challenge briefing. So TEN new players in a day exhaust the entire budget, after which no persona can message anyone, including the 11th player's tutorial mail. Essential transactional mail should not share a budget with discretionary faction chatter. Fix: exempt system/tutorial mail, or make the cap per-persona-per-player. (Discovered because it blocked verification. The predicate fix did NOT cause it — old and new predicates matched all 20 identically.)"
    "U3d-follow-3 (DESIGN DECISION, YOURS): TWO accounts now exist per faction character — npc_steele owns 8 servers and sends U3d intrusion warnings, while 'Commander Steele' is the persona account for faction mail and forum posts. A player would see messages from both, one named 'npc_steele', which is not a name anyone should see in an inbox. Options: (a) point resolveAiPersonaUserId at the NPC owner for that persona's faction — simplest, but the ugly name persists; (b) rename the NPC owners in npcOwnership.ts to display names, which unifies AND reads correctly, at the cost of migrating existing ownerId rows. Recommendation: (b)."
  ]
}

@npc_reactions {
  @arity_bug_fixed [
    "PREREQUISITE FOUND FIRST — the existing reaction path was DEAD. hackService.alertFactionAI called personaService.onFactionServerHacked({...object}) against a 4-POSITIONAL signature (personaService.ts:775), so serverFactionId/attackerUserId/detected were all undefined, the lookup threw, and the faction AI NEVER learned about a high-evidence intrusion."
    "WHY IT SURVIVED: that call site used getService<any>(PERSONA_SERVICE) — the `any` erased the contract so tsc never saw it. The OTHER call site (hackService.ts:1420) was typed and correct all along. Both the call and the `any` are now fixed."
    "VERIFIED BY EXECUTION: AIKnowledge rows for Commander Steele now grow on a breach, with attacker and server recorded. Concrete instance of the Phase 5 'arity bugs' item and the audit's getService<any> theme."
  ]

  @design [
    "npcReactionService.ts hooks the two upper rungs of hackService.triggerCounterMeasures, adding an 'owner_contacted' countermeasure. Resolved lazily via the container and returns false rather than throwing — NPC flavour must never break the hack pipeline."
    "SENDER IS THE SERVER OWNER (npc_* user), not a separately-minted ai_* persona user. The entity that owns the box is the entity that contacts you. This also dodges getAIUserId's duplicate-identity problem, which would have created a second 'Commander Steele' distinct from the npc_steele that owns the servers. The AIPersona is used ONLY as the voice (systemPrompt)."
    "SILENCE IS MEANINGFUL: below evidence 61 nothing is sent, so a clean job feels clean and stealth keeps mattering. 61-80 warns; 81+ escalates tone to match the bounty/trace the ladder already applied."
    "COOLDOWN IS DB-READ (30 min per NPC<->player pair via a Message subject-prefix query), NOT an in-memory Map — a restart cannot hand a player a fresh allowance of warnings. Verified per-NPC not global: a second NPC still reacts while the first cools down."
    "Hand-written voice per faction as safeAI fallback AND as the validator floor, so a garbled generation never reaches a player. npc_sysadmin has no persona and deliberately uses its static voice — the first feedback a new player gets that they were noisy."
    "Attacker username reaches a prompt -> wrapped via sanitizeForPrompt."
    "expectedFormat mirrors exactly what validate reads ({message}) — mismatched field names are the Phase 6b failure mode where good output gets discarded."
  ]

  @my_bug_caught_by_running [
    "The persona lookup filtered on type:'faction_leader', but AIDA leads DarkNet while being typed 'aida' (seed.ts:698). That silently excluded AIDA's 14 servers — THE LARGEST GROUP — from ever getting an AI-voiced reaction. Now matched on FACTION, preferring faction_leader without requiring it."
  ]

  @phase6b_datapoint [
    "Garrison reaction generated a real AI message; the AIDA one fell back. The entire run emitted ONE log line — mine. safeAI's failure path logged NOTHING, even at debug level. This is the 'mostly invisible fallbacks' hypothesis confirmed in miniature: the path is only measurable because it now logs usedAi explicitly. Generalising that instrumentation across the 41+ safeAI call sites is Phase 6b work."
    "Observed AI quality symptom (not a wiring fault): successful output was purple and repetitive — 'there is no escape', 'do not expect mercy', 'the price will be paid'."
  ]

  @open [
    "U3d-follow: messageService.getAIUserId creates AI users with homeIp '127.0.0.1' and User.homeIp is @unique. No user currently holds that IP, so the FIRST persona to need an account succeeds and EVERY ONE AFTER fails on a unique constraint. sendAIMessage is one call away from breaking for all but one persona. Not hit by U3d (which sends as the server owner instead)."
    "ALSO: sendAIMessage's daily-cap query counts only senders whose id startsWith 'ai_', which silently excludes any persona account seeded with a normal cuid — so the 20/day cap is not actually enforced for those."
  ]
}

@server_ownership {
  @invariant [
    "RULE (maintainer decision 2026-08-31): EVERY server must have an owner — a player or an NPC. An ownerless server is UNHACKABLE: hackCommands.resolveHackTarget refuses it ('Target server has no owner'), and the hack pipeline reads the DEFENDER's PlayerProgress (targetProgress.forensics) and bails on !target?.progress."
    "BEFORE: 44 of 59 servers had ownerId null — every Training, faction and NPC box. `hack` worked ONLY against the 15 player homes, making the game PvP-only for hacking and the tutorial's brute-force route impossible. FIXED: 0 ownerless."
    "Half the foundation already existed: 4 NPC users (npc_steele/gh0st/chen/aida) on synthetic 0.0.0.x IPs, role:'npc' already a convention (aiAgentTools.ts:608), and adminApi/stats.ts already counts only role:'player' so NPCs don't inflate player stats. NO MIGRATION NEEDED — `role` already exists on User. But NONE of the four had a PlayerProgress, which was the second half of why NPC servers were unhackable."
  ]

  @implementation [
    "server/prisma/npcOwnership.ts (TRACKED, unlike server/scripts/ which is gitignored). Idempotent; called from seed.ts and runnable standalone via `npx tsx prisma/npcOwnership.ts`."
    "Added npc_sysadmin (0.0.0.5) for neutral public + training infrastructure, deliberately soft at forensics 5 — it is the first defender a new player ever meets."
    "Ownership assigned by RULE, not hand-listing, so later servers are covered: factionId -> that faction's NPC; factionless -> by type (tutorial/public -> sysadmin, underground -> aida, corporate -> chen, government -> steele)."
    "SKIPS and warns on any isPlayerHome server with no owner — claiming it would make it unreachable for its real owner. That is a data fault to surface, not paper over."
    "SEEDING ALONE WAS NOT ENOUGH: darknetDungeonService regenerates dungeons periodically and contentDraftService creates AI servers — both created servers with NO owner, so the invariant would silently rot. Exported resolveNpcOwnerId() is now called at all three remaining creation sites (dungeon, AI draft, admin panel). Returns null rather than throwing so a failure degrades hackability instead of aborting content generation; verified across 9 inputs (incl. unknown type and empty) it NEVER returns null — falls back to sysadmin."
    "Player-home creation paths (auth.ts:109, gameStateManager.ts:793) already set ownerId correctly — untouched."
  ]

  @defender_profiles [
    "forensics: sysadmin 5, gh0st 45, chen 55, steele 65, aida 80."
    "IMPORTANT — only `forensics` actually matters today. calculateHackParameters reads targetProgress.forensics (up to -20% attacker success, +15% detection) and takes stealth from the ATTACKER only. The NPCs' hacking/stealth/cryptography are set for coherence and future mechanics but have ZERO mechanical effect now. Do not tune them expecting one."
    "homeFirewall/homeVault/homeIds/homeHoneypot left at 0 — all those paths are gated on server.isPlayerHome, false for every NPC server, so they would be inert."
  ]

  @side_fix [
    "validateHackAttempt carried a SECOND undocumented floor of `hacking < 10` that silently refused the Hacking 5-9 attempts the new soft gate permits. Now derived from the same constant (20 - SKILL_SOFT_BAND) so the two cannot drift."
  ]

  @open [
    "U3d OPPORTUNITY (not a defect): NPC servers now have owners who could REACT. triggerCounterMeasures already runs for them and personaActionService can generate messages/forum posts, so an intrusion on a faction box could draw a response from that faction's NPC. Not built. IDS/alert paths are isPlayerHome-gated so no junk notifications are generated for NPCs today."
  ]
}

@soft_skill_gates {
  @design [
    "DECISION 2026-08-31 (maintainer): a skill requirement is the BASELINE for an action, not a wall. Below it the player may still attempt, at a penalty proportional to the shortfall. Beyond SKILL_SOFT_BAND (15) it is still refused — 'within reach', not merely curious."
    "KEY INSIGHT: the machinery already existed. calculateHackParameters scales success by hacking and detection by stealth; calculateLayerDifficulty subtracts relevantSkill/25; earned accessLevel scales with skill; and awardExperience grants +1 hacking EVEN ON FAILURE (baseHackGain = success ? ceil(difficulty*2) : 1). The hard gate sat on top of a system that already degraded gracefully, and was the ONLY thing preventing the learn-by-failing loop from resolving its own chicken-and-egg."
    "MODEL (gameBalance.ts 'Soft Skill Gates'): severity = shortfall / SKILL_SOFT_BAND; successRate *= 1 - severity*0.6; detectionRate += severity*0.25. Multiplicative on success (a shortfall scales down whatever edge you had, rather than a flat subtraction that could invert a strong build); additive on detection (fumbling is loud in absolute terms)."
    "Severity is captured WHEN THE SESSION STARTS (HackSessionInfo.skillPenaltySeverity) so mid-session skill gains cannot retroactively soften an attempt."
    "MEASURED: at starting Hacking 10, `hack` (baseline 20) → severity 0.67, success x0.60, detection +0.167. Attemptable from Hacking 5. Full strength at 20."
  ]

  @modes [
    "SkillRequirement.mode defaults to 'hard' DELIBERATELY — a requirement only becomes soft once a penalty is actually wired at the call site, so adding an entry can never silently reduce difficulty."
    "'soft': hack, crack, exploit, backdoor, rootkit. Penalty applied in hackService.calculateHackParameters; severity computed in the COMMAND layer (hackCommands) because only it knows which command was typed — each has a different baseline."
    "'hard': fragment.crack stays hard ON PURPOSE — failure BRICKS the fragment. That is an unrecoverable loss, not a penalty."
    "'unblockable': commands that ANSWER a challenge the game forced on the player. CAUGHT A LIVE SOFT-LOCK RISK: handshake.ack / signal.trace submit the MANDATORY first-visit connection challenge yet were gated at Networking 5 — starting Networking is 10, so it passed by five points of luck. Lowering starting skills would have stranded new players inside an unanswerable challenge. Same for crack.submit, firewall.knock, memory.extract, crack.storm.submit (the layer's difficulty was already set from their skill when generated)."
    "UNGATED entirely: fragments, fragment, backdoor.list — they list things the player already OWNS. A fragment is claimed by cat-ing a file with NO skill check, so a player could hold one at Hacking 10 and be told they lacked the skill to list it. Possession is the real gate; for fragments the AIDA-discovery gate in fragmentCommands already provides it."
  ]

  @presentation [
    "An invisible penalty reads as broken balance (same lesson as S4's ignored-tools notice). The prep card states it: '[!] UNDER-SKILLED: Hacking 10/20 — Success reduced, detection raised. You will still learn from the attempt.'"
    "Help needed a THIRD state beyond locked/unlocked: meetsSkillRequirement is now true for a soft gate you are under-skilled for. New fullyMeetsSkillRequirement() drives a '*' marker plus the legend 'below the recommended skill — usable, but at a penalty'. Without the split, a penalised command is advertised as mastered."
    "Removed a stale '[Hacking 15]' label from backdoor.list's help description after ungating it."
  ]

  @open [
    "U3b IS P0 AND BIGGER THAN U3 WAS: hackCommands.resolveHackTarget refuses ANY server with ownerId null ('Target server has no owner', hackCommands.ts:762). 44 of 59 seeded servers have no owner — every Training, faction and NPC box. So `hack` only works against the 15 PLAYER HOMES: the game is effectively PvP-only for hacking, in a design described as 'mostly solo play with shared objectives'. The tutorial's brute-force route was impossible for a reason unrelated to skill, and every hack_target objective aimed at an NPC server is uncompletable. Root cause: the pipeline reads the DEFENDER's PlayerProgress (targetProgress.forensics) and bails on !target?.progress. FIX = synthesize a defender profile from securityLevel/firewallLevel/encryptionLevel instead of requiring a User row. Land this BEFORE further difficulty tuning — it masks the entire hack economy."
    "U3c: only the hack family converted. decrypt/encrypt/sweep/crack.*/analyze/honeypot/protect/safevault/trace.evade/probe/traceroute/decode/subnet/key.contact/collar.shield/alias:* remain 'hard' because no penalty channel is wired — flipping them without one would be a silent difficulty cut. Each needs its own penalty currency chosen first (fewer files revealed, weaker encryption, longer runtime, higher detection)."
    "NOTE: cryptography and forensics both START at 5, so encrypt/decode (10), analyze (10), decrypt (15) and sweep (15) are ALL refused to a brand-new player today."
  ]
}

@difficulty_knobs_measured {
  @summary [
    "MEASURED 2026-08-31 (U2). Server difficulty knobs are FAR coarser than they look. Every consumer rounds then BUCKETS, so most +1 changes are invisible to players."
    "securityLevel 1..10 yields only 6 DISTINCT player-visible configurations. Indistinguishable classes: sec 1==2, sec 4==5, sec 8==9==10."
    "The ONLY thresholds where +1 is felt: sec 3 (revisits start being challenged, CONNECTION_CHALLENGE_SKIP_THRESHOLD=2), sec 4 (connection tier easy->medium), sec 7 (medium->hard), sec 8 (a 4th hack layer appears via assignLayers duplicating the hardest type)."
    "RULE: tuning a server within an equivalence class does NOTHING. Move it across a threshold or don't bother."
    "firewallLevel is NOT a difficulty knob despite the name. Its ONLY consumer is assignLayers, where it merely REORDERS the same three layer types — never adds, removes, or hardens a layer. It is flavour. Any plan that leans on it for difficulty is mistaken."
    "Hack minigame generators all bucket at d<=3 / d<=6 / d>6 (cipher, port_sequence, memory_trace). Sampling 60 draws at sec1 vs sec2 produced ZERO structural shapes unique to either."
    "Reputation penalty -max(5, sec*2) is clamped by the floor of 5 for all sec<=2. Contest layer count min(5,max(2,ceil(sec/2))) is 2 for all sec<=4."
    "Full threshold map is documented in gameBalance.ts at the top of the Connection Challenges section — read it before tuning any server."
  ]

  @u2_resolution [
    "U2 CLOSED with NO CHANGE, deliberately. The seed's only difficulty edit was Training Firewall securityLevel/firewallLevel 2->1 (seed.ts:871)."
    "The U2 premise was wrong on both halves: (a) it was never compensating for G4 — G4 gates on encryptionLevel, which is 0 for ALL Training servers, so G4 is inert there and securityLevel/firewallLevel are not G4 inputs at all; (b) the 2->1 change is a no-op anyway, since 1 and 2 are the same bucket in every consumer."
    "Reverting and keeping are equivalent, so the seed was left as committed rather than generating churn with zero effect."
  ]
}

@u1_followups {
  @open_decisions [
    "U2b (YOURS TO DECIDE): should the tutorial firewall actually bite? Training Firewall is securityLevel 1, inside the 1==2 dead band, so it is mechanically no harder to CONNECT to than the open training boxes around it — the 'firewall' is fiction. Raising to 3 makes revisits challenge; 4 moves it to the medium tier. Both cut against newbie-friendliness, and per U1 tutorial players reach it by key not by hacking, so hack-layer difficulty never applies to them. Taste call. Pairs with U3."
    "U3 (YOURS TO DECIDE): early-game Hacking gate. `hack` requires Hacking 20 (skillRequirements.ts:39); players start at 10 (auth.ts:97). Hacking skill is awarded BY hacking (hackService.ts:2317) — nearly chicken-and-egg. Sole escape hatch: crack.storm.submit is UNGATED and awards +15 hacking (hackCommands.ts:1255), enough to cross 20 in one solve — but nothing teaches it and it needs a protected file to practise on. Options: lower the gate to 10, grant skill points in early tutorial steps (only step 7 awards any today), or make the crack.storm route discoverable on purpose. INTERACTS WITH U2 — both are early-game difficulty; tuning either alone risks the same 'calibrated against a broken baseline' trap."
    "U5 (Phase 7): command:result.output is `string | string[]` and the client assumes `string`. networkCommands sends `output: challenge.displayText` (array) while everything else sends a rendered string. socket.ts:767 passes it straight into addOutputLine(_, text: string, _) → renders comma-joined, so the handshake table loses line breaks and column alignment in the transcript. NOT player-blocking: the puzzle is rendered correctly by the dedicated panel (Terminal.svelte:1734, {#each displayText as line}). But the no-target-tab fallback calls data.output.substring(0,100), which THROWS on an array. Normalise at the boundary — belongs with the typed socket contract."
  ]

  @method_note [
    "U1's three defects were ALL invisible to static reading and found only by running the flow. The recurring pattern now has four instances: G1 masked by G2, S2 masked by G3, the G3 reward path masked by inventory filtering, and now the tutorial's key route masked by objective crediting. COROLLARY: verify acceptance criteria by execution, and make negative assertions carry positive controls — two of the harness's early PASS rows were vacuous (asserting absence against empty output) until a control was added."
  ]
}

@last_updated {
