  summary_2026_10_07_a4_reads: "A4 part 2 READS DONE: commandModules db.client handles 133 -> 0 and `db` REMOVED from CommandContext (interface.ts) — command modules reach the DB only via services/repositories, resolved with di/resolve accessors. New UserRepository (repositories/userRepository.ts, token USER_REPOSITORY; fixed select, no password hash; findByUsername is case-insensitive and registration now refuses case-insensitive username duplicates). PlayerProgressRepository.get/getMany. serverService.resolveByIpOrName refuses ambiguous names (server names are NOT unique). networkTopologyService.playerKnowsServer is the one rule for revealing a server by id (tap, share_intel). Commands resolve relative paths against session.currentDirectory — terminals[i].currentDirectory is never updated by cd. See PLAN.md A4 part 2 READS."
  summary_2026_10_07_a4_writes: "A4 part 2 WRITES DONE: commandModules direct db.client writes 30 -> 0 (MAX pinned at 0) (ratchet scripts/verify-a4-command-writes.ts — first reported 0 falsely: 4 player_progress writes hid as tx.* callbacks in defenseCommands.purchaseDefense, now playerProgressRepository.purchaseHomeDefense, which also fixes defense purchases never pushing the balance; parser-based comment stripping in scripts/lib/strip-comments.ts; WRITE regex now spans line-split chains). New services: AccountAdminService (role/mute/ban/resetpw — role caches invalidated on HTTP + live sockets, mute enforced in messages/forum), BountyService (claim/complete). Bounties were UNCLAIMABLE (listing shows a 16-char id, claim did findUnique on the 25-char cuid) and, behind that, raced on claim, double-paid on completion, accepted pre-bounty access as proof, ignored expiry; postBounty dedupe was blocked forever by a lapsed active bounty (nothing writes status expired). New system helpers: fileService.setNodeFlags, fileService.purgeNode, fileService.markDownloaded (merges), fileService.moveNodeSystem (P2002 -> NAME_TAKEN), fileService.redeployDecoys, chatService.addContact/removeContact, fileCommands saveDownload (one copy of the download routine; provenance by the created node id, not a filename lookup), hackService.recordAbortedProbe, keyFragmentService.brickFragment, playerProgressRepository.announceCommittedCredits. Open questions in PLAN.md A4 part 2."
  summary_2026_10_07_a8_terminal: "A8 CLOSED. Terminal.svelte 2,867 -> 2,370: handleSubmit decomposed (services/commandResult.ts) and the status bar extracted to TerminalStatusBar.svelte (markup + 41 CSS rule groups incl. those inside @media blocks + copied keyframes). Svelte CSS can't be moved to a plain .css file without losing scoping, and @keyframes are scoped per component. Client verification method that worked: full getComputedStyle maps of every element, before vs after, at each breakpoint (1280/1000/760/470), in ONE dev-server session. Browser-testing a fresh account: register through the app's own API from page JS with a random in-page password. GOTCHAS: Vite dev cache can serve a deleted-and-recreated .svelte file's OLD css; ~20 page reloads exhaust the /api rate limit (restart backend)."
  summary_2026_10_07_bus: "In-process bus contract enforced by scripts/verify-bus-contract.ts (twin of check-socket-contract.ts): every this.emit has a listener, every listener an emitter, every dynamicContent hook a processEvent feed and vice versa — no allowlist. Matching is by NAME; emitter identity for index.ts is covered by verify-phase7-a8-bootstrap. LISTENER FORMS: .on/.once, gameStateManager.subscribe(emitter, \"x\"), and `for (const evt of [\"a\",\"b\"])` loops — a literal .on search misses the last two. Deleted 16 dead emits; wired backdoor:discovered/dungeon:conquered/file honeypot/story:post-read. PlayerPresenceService, InventoryService, GameStateManager, CommandProcessor are no longer EventEmitters. HARNESS GOTCHAS: CLAUDE.md's comment-strip idiom misses TRAILING // comments; the suite shares the dev server's /api rate limit (~2 full runs per 15 min) — run-verify.sh now names RATE-LIMITED/ECONNREFUSED causes."
  summary_2026_10_07_a8_auth: "A8: AuthService extracted (services/authService.ts 488; routes/auth.ts 563 -> 138 thin adapters; token AUTH_SERVICE). Faction/topology/IP services resolved lazily via await import so their failures stay non-critical and no static di/container edge is added. BUG FIXED first (43a13f5): same-second JWTs were identical -> 409 on login-after-register, same-second refresh signed users out; now jwtid per token. verify-phase7-a8-auth.ts needs the dev server and uses 10 failed logins per run: the per-IP lockout (30) is in server memory, so restart the server rather than running it 3x on one process."
  summary_2026_10_07_a8_hack: "A8: hackService 2,739 -> 1,799 + hackCountermeasureService (581; triggerCounterMeasures, owner/faction alerts, NPC reaction, postBounty, rep penalty, sendSecurityAlert; EMITS bounty:posted — listen on hackService.countermeasures) + hackScoring (296; pure functions + BASE_*/METHOD_DIFFICULTY/TOOL_EFFECTIVENESS) + hackSessionStore (136; activeHacks/sessionTimers maps + DB persistence; HackService keeps getters so its code is unchanged). HARNESS RULE learned twice in one day: after a split, a source-reading harness must read the whole DOMAIN (all files it spans) — reading one file makes NEGATIVE checks pass vacuously. Also: grep for a filename misses harnesses that build the path from a template literal."
  summary_2026_10_07_a8_forum: "A8: forumService 3,102 -> 930 + forumAccessService (886) + forumModerationService (626) + forumContentService (793), DI singletons exposed as forumService.access / .moderation / .content. Groups chosen by measuring what each method reaches via `this` — content touched only io/logger/pickRandom and nothing called into it. LIVE_FEED_MEMBERSHIP (static) moved to ForumAccessService; all four consumers now go through access. Harness gotcha: stubs on `(forums as any).io` must now target `.access.io`. Findings parked in PLAN A8: 3 dynamicContent hooks never forwarded (honeypot/backdoor/dungeon) and story:post-read with no consumer."
  summary_2026_10_07_a8_content: "A8: serverContentService split 2,947 -> 1,554 + serverContentTemplates.ts (973: types, rosters, secrets, STATIC/DEFAULT_CONTENT, generateRoleContent, generateEncodedFiles) + serverContentPrompts.ts (461: LORE/AMBIENT/MISSION prompts, pick* helpers, build*Prompt). NetworkContext moved to templates (no importers of the old path). Verified with a FUNCTION fingerprint, not just a data hash: verify-phase7-a8-server-content.ts runs every moved function over a 228-case matrix with seeded Math.random (reset per case) and frozen Date; baseline a8-server-content.baseline.json recorded against the UNMOVED code. HARNESS GOTCHA: a determinism helper that restores the ORIGINAL Math.random breaks when calls nest — restore the PREVIOUS value; the harness's own determinism precondition caught it (104 cases differed between two runs of identical code)."
  summary_2026_10_07_a9: "Phase 7 A9 CLOSED — see PLAN.md A9. Deleted the zero-caller applyContentPlanViaFileService (superseded: keyless LOCKED encrypted files are the deliberate R9 model). The real find: system-provisioned files were owned by user.findFirst() with no orderBy — an arbitrary user — in TWO places (applyContentPlan, and ensureBaseFilesystem's DI-unavailable fallback). createdBy grants owner access BEFORE the requiredAccessLevel gate. Now null, matching all 1,770 existing system files and fileService.initializeFileSystem. Also: `report file` no longer stores a locked file's plaintext preview; probe and whois reports de-duplicated (buildProbeOutput, formatWhois), proven byte-identical old-vs-new because the golden master covers NEITHER command. LESSONS: (1) a negative control that stayed GREEN exposed that the A9 harness was exercising initializeFileSystem, not the fallback I had fixed — forced the fallback by re-registering FILE_SERVICE with a throwing factory, and proved it ran via its deterministic root id `root_<serverId>`. (2) Two of my own controls were vacuous (sed did not match, so nothing changed); assert the edit applied before reading the result."
  summary_2026_10_07_escalation: "S7 follow-up, maintainer decision: abandoned moderation re-checks now go to the admin review queue — see @ai_resilience.@moderation_recheck_queue.@admin_escalation. Filed as SYSTEM reports into the existing MessageReport/PostReport queues. Escalation is a REQUIRED gate parameter, so no caller can skip it. Also closed two silent paths found on the way: an unresolvable moderation service published with no re-check at all, and graceful shutdown dropped every pending re-check. Remaining named gaps: crash, and a failed escalation write (counted). 56/56, 950 checks, golden 23/23."
  summary_2026_10_07_tooling: "Two lying checks found while running the A10 suite. (1) server/scripts/run-verify.sh's CHECKS tally used awk -F'[ /]' and summed $5 — with a single-char separator, ' / ' yields two EMPTY fields, so the fail total was ALWAYS 0. Every 'N pass, 0 fail' quoted in earlier summaries came from it (exit codes, and therefore SUITE ok/fail, were always honest). Now extracts with sed; proven against a known-red log set (old: 0 fail, new: 1 fail). (2) verify-phase5-o9-timers matched setInterval( against raw source, so a comment quoting 'setInterval(fn, NaN)' made environment.ts a 'timer owner'. Now strips comments; true inventory is 24 owners, not 27 (lifecycle.ts and shutdownTimers.ts were also comment-only). Negative-controlled with a real un-stopped timer."
  summary_2026_10_07_a10: "Phase 7 A10 CLOSED — all 18 dead gameBalance constants resolved; see PLAN.md A10. The four groups parked 'for a decision' needed none, and two hid defects. (1) renice never updated a running process's detectionModifier: reniceProcess computed applyPriority's full result but wrote back only cpuCost/duration, so `renice -10` on a hack prep = 2x speed, zero detection penalty. Found by asking why a zero-caller getDetectionModifierForPriority existed — it duplicated the caps the harness was checking. (2) aiSchedulerService parsed AI_INTERVAL_HOURS etc. with raw parseInt; NaN -> setInterval(fn, NaN) ~1ms tight AI loop. Now via config/getEnvNumber + validateConfig (>= 1h). Also: CRITICAL_EVIDENCE_THRESHOLD=80 (3 gates); BOUNTY_EVIDENCE_THRESHOLD derived =+1 (postBounty only reachable in the critical block); DETECTION_* constants are FRACTIONS now; DETECTION_STEALTH_REDUCTION corrected 8% -> 0.20 to match the code. GOTCHA: under `tsx -e`, `import()` of a TS module goes through CJS interop — named exports land on `.default`."
  summary_2026_10_07: "S7 moderation fail-open closed at the queue level — see @ai_resilience.@moderation_recheck_queue. The `unavailable` branch was correctly documented as a deliberate fail-open with a queued re-check; the undocumented part was that the re-check rode a 20-slot queue shared with six other AI callers and had FOUR ways to be dropped, three of them invisible (overflow evicted the oldest regardless of kind; the 10-min age purge had no log and no counter whatsoever). Worst of the set: `await onVerdict(...)` sat inside a bare `catch {}`, so moderation returning UNSAFE and the hide action then throwing was swallowed with zero record. Fixed: critical-flagged entries are evicted last, all four drop paths go through one `abandon()` chokepoint with an ERROR log and a `moderationRechecksAbandoned` metric on the health endpoint, and parse is split from enforcement. 55/55 harnesses, 890 checks, golden master 23/23 unchanged."
  summary_2026_10_06: "REVIEW #3 — see @review3_2026_10_06. 10 findings -> 15 -> 21 across three reviews, half of each round being defects in the previous round's fixes. Stopped fixing and changed approach: the rate limit on `authenticated` is REVERTED (shared budget with typing indicators, so exhausting it and reconnecting left a live socket in no rooms), ~60 lines of comment archaeology in client/src/stores/gameState.ts are DELETED rather than corrected a fourth time, and eight real defects are fixed with an individual negative control each — backdoor drains wiped on logout, an unstoppable sweep timer, a tap rollback that deleted the player's existing tap, a check-then-act war cap that awarded 400 against 200, a flush that discarded slices on a transient failure, live notifications never marked read (permanent replay backlog), a forum ban that did not evict the socket, and the live feed streaming proxy-only forums."
  summary: "2026-09-25 ORPHAN AUDIT CLOSED — see @audit_closed_2026_09_25, @finishing_the_orphan_work, @code_review_2026_09_25, @state_push, @event_delivery, @orphan_audit_2026_09_24. Everything still open is now wired, refused with a reason, or filed to Phase 8 with one: four orphaned socket events got listeners, forum rooms are actually joined (they never were — the emit and the listener matched by NAME and the room had no members), updateWarScore got its first producer, passive resource drain runs, rejectDraft was unreachable because its route had inlined a duplicate, and the client REST connect path (404, no callers) is gone. THE GOLDEN MASTER CAUGHT the drain wiring as a real behaviour change and the diff was exactly the intended effect — that is the strongest evidence in this pass. Still deliberately open: hack alerts + handleNPCReply (Phase 8), getTraceDuration (INVERTED, refused), backdoor drains not restored at boot, 9 direct player_progress writers, and this file being three phases behind."
  previous: "2026-09-24 CODE REVIEW PASS 3 OF PHASE 5 — ELEVEN FINDINGS, NINE OF THEM DEFECTS IN PHASE 5'S OWN FIXES. The pattern is now unmistakable: a correct fix repeatedly exposed something worse underneath, and three fixes were WRONG IN A WAY THEIR OWN GREEN HARNESS CERTIFIED. SERVER: (1) download emitted a success AND a failure message for one failed attempt — the success emit was gated on `context.io` alone, never on `result.success`, and the R12 comment claiming TypeScript had verified the nesting was false; (2) R12's `mission:failed` emit made ABANDONING a story mission walk the arc's failureBranch and set storyArc.status='failed' PERMANENTLY, while abandonMission returns the row to the pool as available — abandon now only writes a ledger entry, expiry still advances the arc; (3) the no-successor epoch branch was written as status:'fired', success:true, permanently consuming an event the scheduler only re-reads while 'pending'. CLIENT: (4) the 'urgent'->'critical' rename MISSED socket.ts:879, the one site that maps server severity — CRITICAL alerts mapped to a value matching no sound, colour or CSS rule while non-critical got 'high' and did; the .notif-priority.urgent CSS rule was missed too (the template interpolates the raw priority into the class name); (5) the mail badge counted $unreadCounts.total — every type — so one unread `game` notification rendered '📧 1' / '0 mail messages'; (6) clearNotifications() assigned to `$:`-derived variables and marked NOTHING read, so R13's removal of the other writer left the badge PERMANENTLY UNCLEARABLE; (7) `reconnection: false` regressed reconnection from socket.io's Infinity to a 5-attempt ~31-second ceiling — any drop longer than that was permanent; now indefinite with the delay capped at 30s; (8) awaiting reconnect() brought a dead reject path to life that unwound past terminalTabsStore.initialize(), leaving a terminal with ZERO TABS. WITHDRAWN: R12-d's per-tab cwd fix. getSessionContext had ZERO CALLERS and preferred activeTerminal.currentDirectory — a field NO code path updates after session setup, since `cd` writes only session.currentDirectory. Wiring it up would have resolved every relative path against the connect-time home directory, ignoring every cd. (For the record: `switchTerminal` DOES maintain activeTerminalId from sockets/handlers.ts:825 — it is the DIRECTORY that is unmaintained, not the id.) The real fix — `cd` writes the issuing tab's field, then all 26 reader sites move together — is filed in PLAN.md; the harness now asserts the precondition, so it flips to failing when the real fix becomes safe. METHOD: the structural guard that certified R12-d (/activeTerminalId/.test(src)) matched the COMMENT explaining the fix, not the fix. That trap fired FOUR times in Phase 5. Harness guards now strip comments before matching, and every guard added this pass was negative-controlled by reintroducing the bug and confirming the check fails. DB RESET VERIFIED (2026-09-24, maintainer ran npm run db:reset): schema pushes clean and the seed runs from scratch — 8 users, 58 gameServers, 94 serverLinks, 665 fileSystemNodes, 33 shopItems, 4 factions, 5 AIPersonas, 1 narrativeEpoch (Genesis). ALL 162 HARNESS CHECKS PASS AGAINST A FRESHLY SEEDED DATABASE, which is a stronger result than the accumulated dev DB they had always been run against. THE FRESH SEED EXPOSED ONE FINDING the old DB hid: KnowledgeTopic and PlayerKnowledge are DEAD SCHEMA — tables with no code at all (see @knowledge_and_player_missions). DOC VERIFICATION PASS (same day): every checkable claim in CLAUDE.md was re-verified against source. 12 corrections — the biggest being that bug-shape #4's canonical example (mission:failed has no emitter) is now FIXED BY THIS VERY PASS, and that BOTH of CLAUDE.md's 'two inverted entries that would cause harm' have already been corrected in this file (offers-are-per-player now stated correctly at L2128; the prisma-migrate-dev hazard now carries an explicit setup_warning at L1884). CLAUDE.md's own 'assume fixed' rule caught its own doc. Also corrected: 2 of the 9 player_progress writer citations had drifted onto COMMENTS (missionService 1362→1389, tutorialService 997→1023) — which is how a stale line number disguises itself as a fixed bug; missionObjectiveTypes' three disagreeing self-counts (27/24/24) are ALL wrong, the map holds 37; asyncHandler 52→53; harnesses 30→36 total with 5 tracked. HARNESS DEFECT FIXED: R12-e read ambient dev-DB epoch state and called handleAdvanceEpoch() — a STATE-MUTATING service method — against it. That is what completed the world's only epoch earlier in this phase. It now builds a fixture at order -1000 (selected ahead of the seeded Genesis epoch, which storyProgressionService RECREATES ON EVERY DI BOOT — so 'no epochs exist' is not a state that survives), asserts every pre-existing row is byte-identical afterwards, and deletes by id in a finally that REPORTS cleanup failure instead of swallowing it. Suite: 162 checks green across 12 harnesses (scripts/verify-phase5-*.ts, + verify-phase5-review3.ts new this pass); server tsc and svelte-check both clean. NOTE: the client build now REQUIRES VITE_API_URL — vite.config.ts fails the build rather than silently baking in localhost:3001. Previous: 2026-09-24 PHASE 5 LANDED — eight commits, c5a4092 through b452b7f, committed as WIP on branch `test`. R5 typed all 51 getService<any> sites and fixed the three bugs they hid (c5a4092); R4 made trace completion reachable and evade matter (8293225); P5-NEW restored the topology-empty discovery fallback (d476d64); R6 bound every socket to the session and fixed room membership (5628efa); R7 gave Mission.timeLimit ONE unit — seconds — and ONE conversion point, utils/missionTime.ts (4a33a0c), then made isBonus real: a typed column, schema.prisma:1916, not a Json field (c8f6216); the Phase 5 code review fixed six regressions the phase itself had introduced (c6fd233); R9 stopped encryption from destroying files (b452b7f). RE-VERIFIED AGAINST SOURCE 2026-09-24, all four CLAUDE.md invariants: getService<any> is at ZERO (the 3 textual hits are comments — serverService.ts:59, aiAgentTools.ts:424, hackService.ts:2135); timeLimit has zero unit violations outside missionTime.ts (the remaining `* 1000` sites are a DIFFERENT field — minigame/challenge limits); requiredObjectivesComplete has exactly ONE implementation, utils/missionCompletion.ts:32 — NOT missionTime.ts, as an older entry implies — with both former rivals now delegating (missionService.ts:828, playerMissionRepository.ts:508-513); and socket room operations cover all of a user's sockets (io.in(`user:${userId}`).socketsJoin at gameStateManager.ts:744, socketsLeave at :821, the only two in server/src). ALSO CORRECTED THIS PASS: the @audit_2026_08_30 verdict is obsolete (G1-G7 fixed), G5 is definitively FIXED, and SECURITY_CLEANUP.md's git-history claim is disproven — no secret was ever committed. Previous: 2026-09-23 CODE REVIEW OF THE WHOLE UNCOMMITTED DIFF — NINE REAL BUGS, every one in code already reported as verified with green harnesses; nine fixed, one pre-existing filed to Phase 5. TWO NEW STANDING DECISIONS (15, 16): a code review runs BETWEEN EVERY PHASE, and nothing is committed until Phase 7 passes and the review backlog is clear. THE DOMINANT SHAPE, four of nine: 'I fixed one half of a pair and left the other' — copyNode but not moveNode, the credit check but not the stack cap, the joined server's count but not the abandoned one, the `if` branch but not the `else`. A test of the working half PASSES, which is why harnesses structurally cannot catch it. WORST: incrementObjective skipped the lock while writeMission writes currentCount ABSOLUTELY — reproduced, a +7 during a concurrent boolean update left the count at 0, reopening the exact lost-update class D3 pass 2 was built to close. ALSO: completeMission lost its payability guard (mission burned, unpaid, unretryable); assertPositiveInt threw on ZERO, burning a zero-reward bounty and silently paying nothing on AI-authored fractional dungeon rewards; creditObjective wrote `true` into string-target objectives, corrupting them permanently; addExperience emitted duplicate level-up toasts; moveNode leaked raw Prisma constraint text to the player. I ALSO CONTRADICTED MY OWN DOCSTRING again — connectToServer claimed the count 'cannot drift' while never resyncing the abandoned server. RISK RECORDED on decision 16: the tree is 32+ files and we lost power mid-session today; local WIP commits on branch `test` were offered as a mitigation that does not ship anything. Suite after fixes: progress-repo 29/29, d3-lock 23/23, gate 15/15, tutorial 11/11, schema 18/18, D6 8/8, G3 19/19, shop 10/10, P0 4/4, provisioning 5/5, tsc 0, eslint 0, boot 0 errors. See @phase3_code_review. Previous: PHASE 3 CLOSED — gate rewritten and passed, one maintainer-owned leg open. The 09-01 gate demanded concurrency checks 'by hand, two browser tabs'; harnesses with NEGATIVE CONTROLS do strictly more, because two tabs cannot demonstrate a race is REPRODUCIBLE, which is the only thing that makes a passing assertion mean anything. Provisioning-from-empty was proven on a THROWAWAY database (70 tables, 18 FKs, 5 uniques, 10 D9 indexes from schema.prisma alone, then dropped) instead of wiping the dev DB — same property, 134 users and the D3 backfill preserved. STILL OPEN, owner maintainer: `npm run db:reset` end-to-end, since running prisma/seed.ts is blocked here by the same safety check D2 hit; the db push half IS proven, only the seed is unobserved. Deferred by decision: take limits (measurement argument), D4's CHECK and the FileSystemNode partial root index (both raw SQL, both land with migration adoption). Previous: D7 UPSERT CONVERSION DONE — PLAN.md named ONE site; there were 39 (7 inventoryItem + 32 fileSystemNode). THE WORK WAS CLASSIFICATION, NOT CONVERSION: merge where re-running is the intent (provisioning, content injection, dungeon regen, reward grants), ERROR where the player must know — touch/mkdir/cp now report FILE_EXISTS/DIRECTORY_EXISTS/DESTINATION_EXISTS rather than a generic failure, since an upsert there would silently overwrite a file nobody asked to replace (`cp` had no name check AT ALL). TWO PLAYER-VISIBLE DEFECTS FOUND BY CLASSIFYING, not by the constraint: the honeypot decoy generator CHARGED THE PLAYER 5000c AND THEN REPORTED FAILURE when a decoy name collided with a real downloaded file (no try/catch there or in either caller); and serverContentService's BARE CATCH was the thing building duplicate roots — it meant 'FileService not registered' but swallowed every failure, so a part-way init fell through to a path that built a SECOND root. ROOTS NEEDED A DIFFERENT MECHANISM: the unique does not cover them (null parentId, NULLs distinct) and Prisma types the compound parentId as non-nullable, so the two real provisioning paths now use a DETERMINISTIC id `root_<serverId>` and let the PRIMARY KEY resolve the race. Deliberately not converted and each checked: auth (unreachable inside a tx), admin/draft creates (409 is the right answer), and 7 guarded transient roots — whose real fix is a partial index that, like D4's CHECK, waits for migration adoption. A SCRIPTED EDIT NEARLY BOTCHED IT: a regex matched 2 of 5 darknet roots and the `assert n == 5` fired before the write — assert expected counts in every multi-site script. Suite green: gate 15/15, provisioning 5/5, P0 4/4, G3 19/19, shop 10/10, schema 18/18, D6 8/8, progress-repo 26/26, d3-lock 22/22, tutorial 11/11, tsc 0, eslint 0, boot 0 errors. See @phase3_d7_upserts. Previous: D3 PASS 2 COMPLETE — mission state now lives in PlayerMission + PlayerMissionObjective, backfilled, with the repository's external contract UNCHANGED so pass 2 touched no caller. That is what the two-pass split bought. THE FK FOUND A REAL PROBLEM: 78 of 299 blob entries (26%) pointed at Mission rows that no longer existed — all `active`, all ALREADY non-functional since completeMission throws on a missing Mission — skipped and reported by the backfill, and onDelete:Cascade stops the class recurring. BOTH DEFERRED ITEMS CLOSED: findExpired is one indexed query where pass 1 scanned every player, and incrementObjective is a relative UPDATE so the ~18 callers that computed `objective.current + n` outside the lock now pass a delta; the harness's negative control measures the old shape landing 1 OF 25 concurrent credits — a 96% loss rate, worse than the audit implied. TWO HARNESSES BROKE AND NEITHER WAS A REGRESSION: tutorial-altpath and gate-phase1 both seeded and read playerProgress.missionProgress DIRECTLY, reaching behind the app's API, so the storage change broke them for reasons unrelated to what they assert; both now go through the repository and are green (11/11, 15/15). The folklore answer — 'tutorial is flaky under load' — would have buried a real signal. Suite green: d3-lock 22/22, progress-repo 26/26, schema 18/18, D6 8/8, tutorial 11/11, gate 15/15, P0 4/4, G3 19/19, shop 10/10, provisioning 5/5, tsc 0, eslint 0, boot clean. Phase 3 now has only D7's upsert conversion, take limits and D4's deferred CHECK left. See @phase3_d3_pass1. Previous: D3 PASS 1 COMPLETE — all 64 missionProgress call sites are on PlayerMissionRepository; only 3 row CREATIONS still touch the blob. Races closed per method: completeMission is THE DOUBLE-REWARD SITE and is now an atomic claim (exactly one caller can transition the mission, so exactly one can pay — ordered claim-then-pay deliberately, since pay-first is repeatable and therefore exploitable); acceptMission's double-accept also RESET startedAt, moving the expiry window; missionGenerator merged into a snapshot taken before minutes of AI generation, wiping everything the player did meanwhile. THE REENTRANCY GUARD IS THE STORY: the mutex is not reentrant, so a nested call would hang a player permanently with no error — my first guard used a Set of held users and THE CLASS'S OWN HARNESS CAUGHT IT, because a Set cannot distinguish re-entry from a legitimately QUEUED concurrent caller, which is the whole point of the queue; it is AsyncLocalStorage now. TWO THINGS DEFERRED TO PASS 2 RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER: userIdsWithMissions is only a partial fix to the unbounded sweep read, and updateObjective's callers pass an ABSOLUTE value computed from a `current` they read outside the lock, so same-objective counters can still collapse — no lock can reach that, it needs relative increments. Suite green: d3-lock 13/13 (negative controls still reproduce the old loss), tutorial 11/11 over real sockets, progress-repo 26/26, schema 18/18, D6 8/8, gate 15/15, P0 4/4, G3 19/19, shop 10/10, provisioning 5/5, tsc 0, eslint 0, boot clean. See @phase3_d3_pass1. Previous: 2026-09-23 PHASE 3 STEP 4 PASS 1 STARTED + THE N+1s CLOSED. THE BOOT N+1 DID NOT EXIST: provisionAllUnpopulatedServers AND provisionAllNetworkServers both had ZERO callers anywhere in the repo — ContentQueueService.enqueueAllUnpopulated replaced them and ALREADY batches with groupBy — so the queue rewrite had fixed it long ago and left two orphans holding the old pattern; deleted 94 lines rather than optimise dead code, which is the exact trap the D9 survey had warned about one item earlier. The `ls` N+1 was ALREADY FIXED by Phase 1's N2 work; the plan's checkbox was just stale. The expiry-sweep read is deliberately left to D3 pass 2, which replaces the query rather than tuning it. D3 SCOPED HONESTLY: 64 touchpoints across 7 files and NO SEAM — every one inlines `(progress.missionProgress as any) || {}`. Blob shape measured from the live DB (89/101 players, max 6 missions, 285 entries, 415 objectives). Doing it in TWO PASSES so two sources of truth never coexist: pass 1 = repository over the existing blob (done), pass 2 = swap internals to tables + backfill. The lock is a QUEUE, deliberately NOT the sessionLocks pattern the plan suggested extending — that one REJECTS when contended, which would silently drop a reward. checkExpiredMissions converted, and the fix has TWO halves: one critical section AND moving the side effects out of it, since the audit log + mission.update awaits are what made the window two round trips wide. Harness 11/11 whose negative controls reproduce the double-reward mechanism exactly (the old shape leaves m1=active, the completion entirely lost). Suite green: d3-lock 11/11, progress-repo 26/26, schema 18/18, D6 8/8, tutorial 11/11, gate 15/15, P0 4/4, G3 19/19, shop 10/10, provisioning 5/5, tsc 0, eslint 0, boot clean with 0 error lines. See @phase3_d3_pass1. Previous: 2026-09-23 PHASE 3 STEP 3 DONE — PlayerProgressRepository. One writer for player_progress, registered in DI and exposed on CommandContext. THE PLAN'S COUNT WAS WRONG: 43 write sites across 22 files, not 27; 24 migrated, and all 19 left were read and accounted for individually (10 are the missionProgress blob = D3/step 4, 4 create the row, 3 outside its remit, 2 documented increments). Design rule: every mutation is ONE statement whose correctness does not depend on an earlier read — spendCredits puts the balance test in the WHERE of the decrement so the rowcount IS the authorization; addSkill clamps with GREATEST/LEAST inside the UPDATE; addExperience raises level monotonically. shopService's `// Re-check credits inside transaction to prevent race conditions` was a LIE — a plain SELECT under READ COMMITTED takes no row lock — and the comment is why nobody looked again. FOUND, NOT IN THE AUDIT: PlayerProgress.level had exactly ONE writer (missionService.grantRewards), so hack/dungeon/decrypt XP never levelled anyone up; measured before escalating — it has never bitten, since the highest real XP is 80 and level 2 needs 100. CORRECTED MYSELF MID-SESSION: I had written that traceService's stealth decrement had no floor; it does (Math.min(2, progress.stealth)) — it is racy, not unfloored. THE HARNESS'S NEGATIVE CONTROLS ARE THE WHOLE TEST: running the OLD patterns reproduces the audit's exact numbers (-200 credits, hacking 101), without which the other 24 assertions could have passed merely because Promise.all failed to interleave. NEW DEFECT FILED TO PHASE 5, deliberately not fixed (decision 11): hackService.ts:1973 calls initiateTrace with 3 args against a 4-param signature, so serverId gets the evidence level and the FK rejects it — that countermeasure has never worked, and it survived behind getService<any>, the THIRD instance of `any` erasing a signature here. Suite green: progress-repo 26/26, schema 18/18, D6 8/8, tutorial 11/11, gate 15/15, P0 4/4, G3 19/19, shop 10/10, provisioning 5/5, tsc 0, eslint 0. See @phase3_progress_repo. Previous: 2026-09-23 PHASE 3 STEPS 1 & 2 DONE. Schema: 3 Mission indexes + 6 more (all derived from real predicates, each citing its call sites), the four missing D10 FKs (+2 Bounty columns the audit missed), R8's skillPoints column, and the K knowledge tables. Code: D10 connections and D6 fragment CAS. HEADLINE — TWICE the live data contradicted the audit and measuring first changed the fix: currentConnections was UNDER-counting (0 stored against 33-38 real rows) because TWO writers with incompatible models run on the same connect and the winner is an in-memory Map that is empty after every boot; and the dominant defect was not in the audit at all — 95 of 95 ServerConnection rows were active with disconnectedAt null on every one, because `connect <ip>` only ever closed the IN-MEMORY session, never the rows. That leak is what let `fragment.steal`'s unscoped findFirst({isActive}) run against a server the player had left, carrying that server's accessLevel past its >=5 gate. TWO MORE AUDIT CLAIMS CORRECTED: 'no code deletes a User today' is FALSE (npcOwnership.ts:201 and seed.ts:499 do — which is why the new FKs are Cascade, not the default Restrict that would have broken the NPC merge), and PLAN.md's own 'R8's column is inert alone' was wrong about the bug (the missing column threw and took the dungeon XP with it; proven by replaying the statement, 0->5000 XP). D6 was fixed in the SHARED helper, so steal and transfer got it too, not just the claim the audit named — and NEGATIVE-TESTED by removing the CAS, which reproduced 2 winners / 2 discovery records / an inflated loser counter. A full index survey found ~70 candidates; only 9 were taken, because the DB is tiny (largest gameplay table is 221 rows) and 70 indexes would be write overhead plus churn for Phase 7. MY OWN HARNESS LIED AGAIN: a FAIL on an FK psql had just proven correct, caused by `message.split('\\n')[0]` — Prisma messages START with a newline — and separately a boot probe that passed on a 404 against a route that does not exist. Suite green: phase3-schema 18/18, d6-race 8/8, tutorial 11/11, gate 15/15, P0 4/4, G3 19/19, shop 10/10, provisioning 5/5, tsc 0, eslint 0. See @phase3_steps_1_2. Previous: 2026-09-01 PHASE 2 DONE + PHASE 3 STARTED; committed a75383d. Phase 1's four audit P0s fixed and verified. Phase 2 shipped CI, the socket contract check, VERIFY.md and the VITE_API_URL guard; Docker and migrations moved to a new Go-live bucket (decisions 13/14). Phase 3: stale gate rewritten (it demanded `prisma migrate deploy`, impossible with no migrations), D7 uniques landed and PROVEN to reject. THE HEADLINE: every defect found in Phases 1-2 was invisible to tsc/eslint/svelte-check and to the static contract check — each was a second code path, an enforcement layer that excluded the fix, or two correct changes cancelling. Running VERIFY.md found three more that all static checks passed clean, including setSocketIO() having zero callers on two services (21 stranded emits, player:levelup among them). Also recorded: FIVE false claims of my own this session, all from writing an intention down as an outcome; and four harness bugs that reported defects which did not exist. See @session_handoff_2026_09_01. Previous: 2026-08-31 PHASE 1 AUDIT — REOPENED. Five adversarial passes (agents told to REFUTE, default to REFUTED when uncertain) over ~25 Phase 1 claims. FOUR P0s open, THREE created by Phase 1 itself: (P0-1) commandProcessor.ts skillGatedCategories covers only hack/network/file/social/alias, so 12 skill requirements are NEVER ENFORCED — including fragment.crack, kept "hard" precisely because failure permanently bricks a unique endgame item; a Hacking-10 player can do it today, and it also makes the sweep.reveal half of the soft-lock fix vacuous. (P0-2) `exploit` passes RAW USER INPUT as the tools array with no ownership check — filterOwnedTools has ONE call site, on `hack` — so `exploit <ip> zero_day` buys +0.25 success for free; this is the hole S4 was written to close. (P0-3) createMissionTargetServer passes ownerId:null and `hack` refuses ownerless servers = the P0 U3b claimed to close, via a 6th creation site. (P0-4) G4 MOVED the encryption failure: mission targets get encryptionLevel = difficulty*10 vs requiredLevel = encryptionLevel*2, so a difficulty-1 mission needs level 20. MEASURED both P0-3 and P0-4 against the live DB: 1 server affected, not the disaster the numbers suggested — CHECK BEFORE ESCALATING. FOUR OF MY OWN DOCUMENTED CLAIMS WERE FALSE: M11 "stricter" note (install_backdoor/breach_server are not in PROVISIONED_OBJECTIVE_TYPES so matchesEntity still matches ANY server), G6 "all sites" (5 comparisons bypass the helper, 3 strict-only), "all five soft commands gate on hacking" (six; analyze is forensics), "no seed_* rows remain" (not an invariant). TWO PHASE 1 ITEMS CANCELLED EACH OTHER: FALLBACK_VOICES is keyed on npc_* usernames that U3d-follow-3 renamed, so EVERY hand-written NPC voice is dead. HEADLINE FEATURE MISSES ITS USE CASE: persona replies are NOT deferred — mailQueue.enqueue has exactly one caller (tutorialService); the real token-gated reply is still inline and immediate. METHOD LESSON: every refutation was a SECOND CODE PATH, an enforcement layer that excludes the fix, or two correct changes cancelling — none visible in the fix's own diff, none caught by a harness (all still pass). Ask "where else does this pattern live?" when closing every phase. See PHASE1_AUDIT.md. Previous: 2026-08-31 PHASE 1 CLOSED. Final suite: gate 15/15, tutorial U1 11/11, G3 hardware 19/19, shop contract 10/10, mission provisioning 5/5, tsc 0, eslint 0. The two closing items were REGRESSIONS IN THE PHASE'S OWN EARLIER WORK, found by an audit rather than a harness: (1) crack/exploit/backdoor/rootkit were declared mode:"soft" with NO penalty wired anywhere — processHackAttempt took no severity param — so U3 had shipped a STRICTLY EASIER game for four commands, violating the doctrine I wrote in the same file; (2) crack.dict/mask/pattern gate on Cryptography but answer a session opened by `crack`, which gates on HACKING, so a player could open a file-crack session they were refused permission to answer. Both fixed. U3c coverage audit found only TWO commands (sweep, hack) have the minigame+process+resources triad that decision 12 needs, so the rest of U3c is Phase 8/9 FEATURE work and was deliberately NOT pulled forward (decision 11). Maintainer instruction recorded: focus each phase, do not skip ahead. See @soft_gate_coverage. Previous: 2026-08-31 G3 LANDED + SHOP STRING BUGS FIXED. Hardware finally changes the rig: verify-g3-hardware.ts 19/19 over the real socket path asserting NUMBERS (level-30 baseline 500/640/250; RAM 640->704 on tier 1, then 768 on tier 2 NOT 832, trade-in +250, net spend 1750). 9 hardware parts + 6 persona tokens moved into SHOP_CATALOG, ending the two-item-universe split; ownership IS installation; supersession trades in at 50% inside the purchase transaction; one refreshComputerSpec helper replaces the omission at all 9 call sites; new `specs` command and a rig refresh before every process readout. Earlier in the turn S1/S5/S15 (three string mismatches) were fixed and proved against the live DB — the old darknet name-lookup returns NULL there. Two harnesses now guard the bug CLASS: verify-shop-contract.ts 10/10 (help example ids, HACK_TOOL_ITEMS, hardware<->spec parity BOTH directions, one part per channel+tier, tokens keeping effect.personaName AND purchasable:false, and a BAN on name-substring matching) and verify-g3-hardware.ts. EVERY contract check was NEGATIVE-TESTED by reintroducing its bug. TWO CORRECTIONS TO MY OWN EARLIER CLAIMS: S20 was WRONG (reseeding would not throw — seed.ts deletes inventoryItem before shopItem), and getCombinedBonuses is an interface not a dead function. Suite green: gate 15/15, U1 11/11, mission provisioning 5/5, tsc 0, eslint 0 errors. See @shop_and_rig. Previous: 2026-08-31 SHOP/RIG READ BEFORE G3 (SHOP_ARCHITECTURE.md). Maintainer stopped me again — \"before making any changes, let's fully read how it works to stop going in circles\" — and it was right again. G3 as scoped in PLAN.md (\"8 call sites omit equippedItemNames\") is the LAST of FIVE broken links; fixing only it would have been a no-op I reported as done. Root cause of most shop defects: TWO DISJOINT ITEM UNIVERSES — the 18-item in-memory SHOP_CATALOG vs the seed_* rows — where every ACTION resolves via the catalog and only DISPLAY reads the table, so seeded items are visible and rejected by every verb. Headlines: crack.protected is unusable by ANYONE (matcher wants \"quantum charge\", item is \"Quantum Decryptor Charge\" — verified by running it; the error even names the item the player is holding, and `use` destroys the 7500c charge for nothing); EVERY example item id in the shop help is fictional so `man buy` teaches a failing command; item effects are displayed and never applied by two summation rules that disagree with each other; the entire MemoryService session lifecycle is dead code so four per-user maps leak for the server lifetime and an active trace's CPU drain is permanent; and free/top show a hardcoded level-1 rig to a level-40 player. Decisions taken: hardware is INSTALLED not equipped (and since it is maxStack-1 non-consumable, OWNERSHIP IS INSTALLATION — no schema change), SHOP_CATALOG becomes the only source of truth. One sub-decision open (supersede-with-trade-in vs stack). 25 defects registered S1-S25. METHOD NOTE: an empty grep nearly became a finding — the shell was at the repo root, not server/, so `grep -rn ... src/` returned nothing and read as \"dead code\"; re-run with absolute paths AND a positive control. Also corrected an earlier claim of my own: getCombinedBonuses is an INTERFACE, not a dead function; the method is getEquipmentBonuses and it has one display caller. See @shop_and_rig. Previous: 2026-08-31 MISSION TIER 1 LANDED (M11-M14, the four argument-level bugs that made ~15 of 39 templates unwinnable). Headline: hackService passed the target USER id into a hook that compares it against a SERVER id, so hack_target could never complete for anyone, ever — the fix was to pass targetServerId, which was already in scope and unused. Both download sites passed fileId:"" (and their (missionIntegration as any) casts were typed at the same time — that cast pattern had already hidden an arity bug once this session). The provisioning gate had drifted from the switch it guards, leaving 3 case arms dead; it is now one exported constant with a source-parsing harness asserting parity. M14 was resolved by AUTHORING intent rather than the registry: install_backdoor was written as a count in 4/5 templates so it BECAME a count, while decode_content was boolean in 9/10 so its outlier was corrected — 134/134 objectives now agree with progressType. TRAP RECORDED: promoting all validateObjective failures to a throw rejects 49 of 134 objectives, because required metadata is legitimately backfilled by provisioning later; only target/progressType is knowable at authoring time. M11 also makes install_backdoor and breach_server STRICTER, not just correct — they were only passing because an unbound matchesEntity returns true for any server. Two of my own mistakes were caught by controls, not review: the new contract harness extracted 0 switch arms (a backticked `switch (obj.type)` in a comment fooled indexOf) so "every arm is gated" passed vacuously; and the U1 harness asserted connection success from OUTPUT TEXT, reporting OK while the player sat on the previous server and every later step ran against the wrong filesystem — it now asserts the ServerConnection row and POLLS, because that row is written after handshake.ack emits. U1 is 11/11 deterministically where it had given 11, then 3, then 3. NEW OPEN ITEM: the world inflates — provisioning mints a target server when it cannot find a suitable one, taking the DB from 38 to 114 servers in a handful of runs with 40 content jobs behind them, and connect awaits ensureReady() so players feel it as a slow connect. Also found UserSession.lastServerId is DEAD SCHEMA (never written in src/). See @tier1_fixes + MISSIONS_ARCHITECTURE.md §7a. Previous: 2026-08-31 PLAN REFRESHED WITH SESSION KNOWLEDGE. New @method_learnings (8 rules that change HOW later phases run) mirrored into PLAN.md; Phase 1 marked substantially COMPLETE with only design decisions left, and its blown estimate explained (scoped from the audit's list, but each fix exposed another layer). Concrete findings routed into the phases that own them: R5 half-done with the count corrected to 53 getService<any> vs 46 typed (typing the call sites IS the arity fix); Phase 6b's 'invisible fallbacks' hypothesis CONFIRMED with npcReactionService's usedAi flag as the pattern to generalise; A3 given U5 as its concrete justification (output is string|string[] and the client's fallback calls .substring on a possible array); a new Phase 6 item for AI spend being unbudgeted on event-driven generation. R8 SHARPENED after verification — the plan's wording was too broad: mission rewards only DISPLAY skill points, and the sole Prisma write is darknetDungeonService intel_package, which throws on the missing column and takes the XP with it, failing 100% of the time it fires (~18% of dungeon conquests grant nothing). MIGRATIONS DEFERRED by maintainer decision until a live DB exists: prisma/migrations deleted, _prisma_migrations dropped, db:deploy removed as a trap, adoption gated in Phase 2. NEW DESIGN (maintainer): missions grant DEFINED skill points (+10 hacking), levelling grants FREE points — spec in Phase 8, and note level-up currently grants nothing and nothing spends points. Code committed as 59b55b4 (32 files); the 13 docs are still UNTRACKED. 2026-08-31 DB RESET + PLAN AUDIT. Migrations rebuilt properly now that a reset was authorised: single 0001_init from schema.prisma, applied via migrate reset (which reseeds), migrate status clean, plus migration_lock.toml and db:deploy/db:reset scripts — that closes D2 from Phase 3. **CORRECTION 2026-08-31 (Phase 2): THIS IS FALSE.** There is NO server/prisma/migrations directory at all, nothing is tracked in git, there is no db:deploy script, and db:reset is still `prisma db push --force-reset && tsx prisma/seed.ts`. Whatever was built was later deleted or never committed. Migrations remain UNADOPTED — treat the Phase 2 GATE item as fully open. Verified by `ls` and `git ls-files`. Replies confirmed to run OUTSIDE the daily AI action budget (verified with actionsToday=13 vs cap 3) and the exemption is now an explicit documented invariant; replies also COALESCE to one pending per sender->player, which closes the abuse hole that exemption would otherwise open. PLAN.md audited against code by two independent passes: all 17 Phase 0/G assertions CONFIRMED, six U-item corrections applied (a migration file that no longer exists, an overstated 'tracked', a superseded fix described as live, three drifted line citations, an under-reported site count, six superseded stubs still counted as open work). Two code defects found and fixed — most notably I had reintroduced the very `as any` untyped-call pattern whose removal exposed the arity bug earlier in the same session. Two latent hazards closed: the ambiguous shared/types.ts barrel is DELETED (removing the ReservedPID bug class rather than mitigating it) and a shadowed `columns` local renamed. HEADLINE PROCESS ISSUE: nothing is committed — 26 modified + 18 untracked paths including 4 new services and all of prisma/migrations/. See @audit_2026_08_31. 2026-08-31 PERSONA MAIL REWORKED: the global 20/day AI message cap is GONE — it could not save AI cost (content arrives pre-generated), it mostly blocked STATIC tutorial/briefing text, and being global it protected nobody's inbox. Replaced with per-recipient+per-sender flood protection (5/hr). The connection-challenge briefing now falls back instead of vanishing silently (it was the one real casualty; tutorial mail always did fall back — correcting an earlier claim). AND replies are no longer instant: new personaMailQueueService + PendingPersonaMail table defers them 45-210s with jitter, generates WHEN DUE rather than inline, and — the key idea — SLIDES delivery later when aiService.getLoad() reports saturation, so backpressure reads as 'still composing' and the queue never skips work. Retries with backoff, always lands a fallback eventually, survives restarts. Also fixed: messagesSent used `update` and threw P2025 for every persona sender, so Prisma logged an error on every persona mail delivered; now updateMany. Schema added via db execute + checked-in migration, NOT migrate dev, because the 0001 baseline is unapplied and migrate would offer to reset the dev DB. See @persona_mail. 2026-08-31 NPC OWNERS RENAMED TO DISPLAY NAMES (U3d-follow-3): one account per character, so an intrusion warning arrives from 'Commander Steele' rather than 'npc_steele'. It was a MERGE not a rename — the persona account already held that name — and the direction was settled by data: the npc_* rows owned all the servers and files while the persona rows had zero references, so the server-owning row wins and adopts the display name plus the persona's canonical email (which makes resolveAiPersonaUserId resolve to it instead of minting a duplicate). reconcileIdentity() is idempotent: renamed 5 / merged 4 on the first run, silent thereafter. Owner == messenger for all four faction characters; server counts preserved. Seed also updated so a fresh DB is correct without the migration. Bonus gotcha recorded: Prisma's startsWith with a trailing underscore is a SQL LIKE wildcard and over-matches silently. See @npc_display_names. 2026-08-31 AI USER IDENTITY FIXED: getAIUserId created accounts with a hardcoded homeIp '127.0.0.1' on a @unique column, so only the FIRST persona could ever get an account and sendAIMessage worked for at most one. It was a DUPLICATED PATTERN across four sites with four different answers (one of which allocated from the PLAYER IP range). All four now go through utils/aiUserIdentity.ts, which allocates from a reserved non-routable 0.0.x.y block deterministically-then-probed, and keys lookups on email rather than editable display names. All 5 personas now deliver. Two adjacent defects fixed: the daily-cap query only counted senders with an 'ai_' id prefix (so seeded persona accounts escaped the cap), and legacy AI accounts carried role:'player' — an AI persona was being counted as a player in admin stats. New open items: the 20/day AI message cap is GLOBAL and includes tutorial mail, so ten registrations exhaust it and the eleventh player gets no onboarding; and two accounts now exist per faction character (npc_steele vs 'Commander Steele') which needs a naming decision. See @ai_user_identity. 2026-08-31 NPC OWNERS NOW REACT TO INTRUSIONS (U3d): new npcReactionService hooks the upper rungs of triggerCounterMeasures so the server's owner contacts the attacker in character. Sender is the SERVER OWNER (npc_*), not a minted ai_* persona user — which also dodges getAIUserId creating a second 'Commander Steele'. Silence below evidence 61 keeps stealth meaningful; DB-read 30-min cooldown per NPC<->player pair survives restarts; hand-written faction voices back up the AI and floor the validator. PREREQUISITE: the existing path was DEAD — alertFactionAI passed a single object to a 4-positional onFactionServerHacked, so the faction AI never learned of any intrusion, and it survived because that call site used getService<any>. Fixed and verified (AIKnowledge now grows on a breach). Also caught a bug of my own by running it: the persona lookup filtered type:'faction_leader' but AIDA is typed 'aida', silently excluding AIDA's 14 servers — the largest group — from AI-voiced reactions. Phase 6b datapoint: one reaction used AI, one fell back, and the whole run logged NOTHING from safeAI's failure path even at debug — 'invisible fallbacks' confirmed. New open items: getAIUserId will throw on the second persona it creates (homeIp '127.0.0.1' is @unique), and sendAIMessage's 20/day cap silently misses seeded persona accounts. See @npc_reactions. 2026-08-31 EVERY SERVER NOW HAS AN OWNER (U3b, P0 FIXED): 44 of 59 servers were ownerless, and `hack` refuses ownerless targets — so the entire NPC world was unhackable and the game was PvP-only for hacking. Maintainer's call to give every server an owner (NPC or user) fixed the data model rather than faking a defender at the call site. Foundation half-existed: 4 npc_* users and role:'npc' were already conventions, but none had a PlayerProgress, which the defence math requires. New tracked server/prisma/npcOwnership.ts assigns owners BY RULE (faction, else type), adds npc_sysadmin for neutral/training infra at a deliberately soft forensics 5, and is idempotent + seed-integrated. Seeding alone was insufficient — dungeon regeneration and AI content creation also made ownerless servers, so resolveNpcOwnerId() is now called at all three remaining creation sites. Only `forensics` on the owner actually affects a hack today (documented, so nobody tunes the rest expecting effect). Also fixed a contradiction it exposed: validateHackAttempt's undocumented `hacking < 10` floor silently refused the Hacking 5-9 attempts the new soft gate permits; now derived from 20 - SKILL_SOFT_BAND. VERIFIED: a fresh player at Hacking 10 can finally `hack 10.10.10.30` — the tutorial's own instruction, which had never worked. See @server_ownership. 2026-08-31 SOFT SKILL GATES (U3) LANDED: skill requirements are now a BASELINE, not a wall — below one you may still attempt at a penalty scaled by the shortfall (severity = shortfall/15; success x(1-0.6*sev), detection +0.25*sev), refused only beyond the band. The supporting machinery already existed (success/detection/layer difficulty all scale with skill, and awardExperience grants +1 hacking even on failure) — the hard gate was the only thing blocking the learn-by-failing loop. New SkillRequirement.mode defaults to 'hard' so nothing eases silently; hack family is 'soft'; fragment.crack stays hard because failure bricks the fragment; in-session challenge submissions are 'unblockable' — which caught a live soft-lock risk where handshake.ack/signal.trace gated the MANDATORY connection challenge at Networking 5 and passed only by five points of luck. fragments/fragment/backdoor.list ungated (they list what you already own). Help gained a third state via fullyMeetsSkillRequirement so penalised commands show '*' rather than reading as mastered. NEW P0 FOUND: U3b — hack refuses every ownerless server and 44/59 seeded servers have no owner, so the ENTIRE NPC world is unhackable and the game is PvP-only for hacking; this masked the tutorial's brute-force route and breaks all NPC hack_target objectives. See @soft_skill_gates. 2026-08-31 U2 CLOSED WITH NO CHANGE: the premise was wrong (G4 gates on encryptionLevel, which is 0 for all Training servers, so it never touched securityLevel/firewallLevel) AND the seed's 2->1 edit is a measured no-op (1 and 2 are the same bucket in every consumer). Measuring it instead produced the useful result: securityLevel 1..10 has only 6 distinct player-visible configurations, with sec 1==2, 4==5, 8==9==10, and real thresholds only at 3/4/7/8 — so tuning within a class does nothing. firewallLevel is not a difficulty knob at all; it only reorders layer types. Both now documented in gameBalance.ts. See @difficulty_knobs_measured. New open decision U2b (should the tutorial firewall bite at all — it currently sits in the dead band). 2026-08-31 U1 DONE: tutorial alt-path verified end-to-end over the real socket path by server/scripts/verify-tutorial-altpath.ts (11/11 PASS). Three defects found by RUNNING it, none visible statically: (1) tutorial step 5 was a hard wall completable by neither advertised route — key route granted access but never credited the objective, hack route gated at Hacking 20 vs the starting 10; fixed with a new breach_server objective type credited by both onHackComplete and a new onAccessGranted hook, deliberately NOT crediting the hack* family; (2) the obvious gain_access fix would have regressed the hack path, since a 'minimal' breach reports success with accessLevel 0 — caught before landing; (3) the tutorial's very first instruction ('connect 10.10.10.1') could not work, because connect needs a direct link and a new home terminal only links to the Internet Exchange. Steps 1/2/5 rewritten to teach the real route and to state the Hacking 20 requirement instead of hiding it. Open follow-ups: U3 (early-game Hacking curve — a design decision, interacts with U2) and U5 (output is string|string[] and the client assumes string). See @u1_followups. Previous: Full 7-dimension audit + remediation plan. Docs: AUDIT_2026-08-30.md (findings), PLAN.md (phases 0-9 + 6b, opens with an 8-entry DECISIONS LOG — read that first), SHELL_DESIGN.md (shell/console architecture + §10a cd/ls navigation audit). Key decisions: no tests until Phase 7 (characterization tests then); the shell tokenizer IS the G8 fix and runs inside Phase 1; shell keybindings win over app shortcuts; audience is shallow-entry/deep-ceiling with elegance rewarded via stealth; scale-out deferred. Two stated pain points driving priority: console feel, and AI content quality — the latter hypothesized to be mostly invisible fallbacks + validators discarding valid output rather than model quality (Phase 6b). Audit headline as recorded 2026-08-30, WITH 2026-09-24 CORRECTIONS: infrastructure is solid, and the core-game-loop half of the verdict is now OBSOLETE — G1-G7 all have fixes in source (see @audit_2026_08_30.verdict for the citations and for what is genuinely still open). Of the five symptoms named here, all five are closed: missions can be accepted, they no longer auto-complete, the shop FK mismatch is fixed by syncCatalogToDatabase at boot, server encryption gates access, and 'any player can read any home server' is FIXED — home servers inherit accessMethod 'hackable' and checkServerAccess grants only to ownerId === userId, enforced on BOTH the terminal path (networkCommands.ts:753) and the socket path (gameStateManager.ts:590). Of the two security holes: server:connect now authorizes through authorizeServerAccess, which ANDs canAccessServer with checkServerAccess and fails closed; the --tools hole was addressed by S4's ownership map, but PHASE1_AUDIT P0-2 reported `exploit` still passing raw input, and that half has NOT been re-verified here. Migrations were later deleted by maintainer decision, not merely gitignored — there is no migrations directory today. Crash path exits 1 (Phase 0). shared/types.js stale artifact means the long-assumed 'Vite bundling issue' behind the ReservedPID duplication was a misdiagnosis. SECURITY_CLEANUP.md's git-history claim is DISPROVEN, not merely doubted: a full history scan shows only four env files were EVER committed (.env.backup, .env.example, .env.test, client/.env.example) and scanning all of them for (API_KEY|SECRET|TOKEN)= yields exactly two lines, BOTH placeholder JWT_SECRETs — no AI_API_KEY or OLLAMA_API_KEY value appears anywhere in git history. NO SECRET WAS EVER COMMITTED, so the git-filter-repo scrub that document prescribes is UNNECESSARY. Whether the live key was ever rotated is unverifiable from here, since server/.env cannot be read in this environment. Previous: Session 3 AI systems: ContentDraftService (draft→approve pipeline creating real entities), ReferenceValidationService (IP/URL backfill + investigation chain generator), EpochSchedulerService (1-min interval, 5 event actions). AI agent loop with 24 tools (19 query + 5 creation) — AI can query full game state (factions, wars, bounties, player activity, hack logs, backdoors, story arcs, fragments, forums, shop) AND create new servers/networks/files/links/posts as drafts. Agent loop integrated into 4 content-creator services (serverContent, storyProgression, personaAction, referenceValidation). Static world topology context injected into 6 more services. Model switched to nemotron-3-super:cloud (120B MoE, 1.6s avg, native tool calling). Rate-limit-aware retry (429 → 5s/15s/45s backoff). Concurrency throttle (max 2 parallel AI requests). IP topology resolver maps all 8 IP ranges to networks/factions/gateways. Auto-link on server creation. Previous: connection challenge rework, 7-step tutorial, admin panel (45 REST endpoints + web UI), responsive terminal, 34 servers, 246 files."
}


@phase4_code_review {
  @summary [
    "Decision-15 review of a94245d..HEAD. 8 finder angles, every survivor re-verified by hand against source."
    "11 defects. NINE were in Phase 4's OWN fixes — bugs written while fixing bugs. 9 fixed, 2 reported as design calls."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-phase4-review-fixes.ts 12/12, scripts/verify-phase4-a9-encrypted-censorship.ts 7/7. Full suite 14 harnesses / 192 checks / 0 fail."
  ]

  @fixed [
    "S10 was applied to ONE of three verify sites. optionalAuth + verifySocketToken still read the shared authCache before jwt.verify. The socket site is worst: io.use is the only gate a socket passes and nothing re-verifies after, so a cache hit on a dead token bought an indefinitely-authenticated connection."
    "handleServerConnect ignored connectPlayerToServer's boolean. The S1 gate refused, then presenceService.playerJoinedServer set currentServerId, added the user to playersByServer, and broadcast presence:player_joined_server to a server they were denied."
    "A9 failed closed only on an unreachable branch. getService = container.resolve, which THROWS — never falsy — so if(!service) was dead. The reachable failure was the constructor's loadRules().catch(()=>{}); seedDefaultRules early-returns before its own loadRules() on every boot after the first, so that swallow was the only load. Empty compiledRules = filter returns input verbatim and reports success. Now ensureRulesLoaded() throws + retries."
    "Encrypted messages skipped censorship: the encrypt branch passed options.content (raw), discarding filteredContent. Pre-existing (17e73b1) but made A9 a no-op for anyone able to encrypt."
    "connectPlayerToServer tore down the previous connection BEFORE authorizing, so a refused server:connect evicted the player from the server they were legitimately on."
    "handleDisconnect ran destroySession unconditionally — closing one of the 4 sockets MAX_SOCKETS_PER_USER blesses killed the session the others were using. Now keyed on isLastSocket from the userSockets index."
    "Rate budget was refunded on reconnect (limiter deleted when the last socket closed). '20 per 10s' became '20 per handshake'. Now expires on IDLE, which bounds the map without refunding."
    "My own report-file S10 fix reported the WRONG FILE: the fallback fired both for 'no path components' (root, the intended case) and 'walk broke partway', so report file /etc/keys.txt on a server with no /etc reported <cwd>/keys.txt while echoing the requested path. Now a single walk; broken path = not-found."
    "canUserReadFile did not enforce reachability. World content is seeded others:5 (READ) + requiredAccessLevel:0, so canRead returned true for any file on any server — including the 'servers they have never reached' the caller's own comment claimed to block."
    "Pre-review self-catch: clientIp() honoured X-Forwarded-For unconditionally. Socket.IO does NOT consult Express trust proxy, so a directly-exposed server let any client forge a fresh per-IP bucket past the S9 cap — the exact hazard the O5 comment two files away warns about."
  ]

  @reported_not_fixed [
    "FACTION-SCOPED CENSORSHIP IS INERT ON 2 OF 5 A9 CALL SITES. filterText skips any rule whose factionId != context.factionId (censorshipService.ts:128). EVERY story rule in the seeded set (AIDA, DarkNet, fragment, Project Echo) carries a factionId. messageService passes {userId} only; systemCommands passes {userId, serverId}. Only forumService populates factionId (from the forum's faction). Verified directly: filterText('...DarkNet...', {userId}) -> wasFiltered:false with all 7 rules compiled. So on the DM and cat paths the ONLY rule that can match is the unscoped SSN pattern, and the censorship_alert events that drive DarkNet discovery never fire from them. Whose rules apply to a DM is a game-design question — NOT settled unilaterally."
    "admin kick invalidates nothing: closes sockets but leaves the JWT, the UserSession row and the auth cache intact, with no cooldown. handleBan does all three. Socket.IO does not auto-reconnect after a server-forced close so a normal client stays out until reload, but the 'modified or stale client' the S3 comment names returns in one handshake. Whether kick should bite is a design call."
  ]

  @confirmed_facts [
    "The client has NO force:disconnect listener anywhere in client/src. The pre-S3 ban therefore did not merely depend on client cooperation — it depended on a handler nobody had written. Destroying the session and broadcasting the reason to every player was the whole of it. The server-side close added in S3 is now the ONLY thing enforcing a ban."
    "SUPERSEDED IN PART — re-verified 2026-09-24. connectPlayerToServer NO LONGER mutates only memory: it calls syncServerConnectionCount, which persists GameServer.currentConnections (gameStateManager.ts:758, :781-789, :814), and the Phase 5 review added the matching resync for the server being ABANDONED, which the docstring had wrongly claimed 'cannot drift'. Still true: neither path creates or deactivates a ServerConnection ROW, so a socket-path connection is still not observable as a connection record; pwd (which falls back to '/' with no session) remains the usable external observable for harnesses."
    "Message model orders by `timestamp`, not sentAt. User create needs `password`, not passwordHash."
  ]

  @harness_lessons [
    "My open() helper armed a 9s watchdog that called socket.close() and never cancelled it on success. Invisible in fast tests; in R3 (which sleeps out a 10s rate-limit window) it killed its own socket and looked exactly like a server-side idle disconnect. Cost a round of debugging the wrong process."
    "R2's first draft asserted on `whoami` — which is NOT a command. 'Command not found' comes back identically with or without a session, so the test passed for the wrong reason. Replaced with pwd, which reads session.currentDirectory and falls back to '/' when the session is gone."
    "The A9 harness's CONTROL failed (an UNENCRYPTED message was not redacted either). That failure — not the test subject — is what surfaced the faction-scoping gap. A failing control is a finding, not an obstacle."
    "Harness summary formats differ: some print '=== N PASS / N FAIL ===', others 'N/N passed', verify-tutorial-altpath prints neither. A grep for one format reported 7 healthy harnesses as 'NO SUMMARY LINE' — an empty grep is not a finding."
  ]
}


@phase5_r5_any_sweep {
  @summary [
    "R5 done as ONE mechanical sweep (the plan's own instruction, after a prior attempt fixed one `as any` then reintroduced the pattern in the same session)."
    "Measured first: 51 REAL getService<any> call sites, not 53 — two of the grep hits are comments naming the pattern. 98 resolves were already typed."
    "Converted all 51 by script, then let tsc find the damage. 4 errors surfaced; 3 were live bugs."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-phase5-r5.ts 11/11. Full suite 17 harnesses / 197 checks / 0 fail."
  ]

  @why_type_only_imports [
    "Nearly every getService<any> site lives inside an `await import(...)` block that exists to BREAK a require cycle. Static imports would have reintroduced them."
    "`import type` is erased at compile time, so it gives the compiler the signature without creating a runtime edge. Verified: server restarted clean, zero ReferenceError / 'cannot access before initialization'."
  ]

  @the_shared_bug_shape [
    "All three live bugs had the SAME shape: a wrong call that could not fail loudly. An `any` hid it from the compiler AND an enclosing catch/fallback hid it from the runtime. That combination is what let them survive."
    "Corollary for testing: asserting 'nothing threw' would have PASSED before every one of these fixes. Each harness check must distinguish 'fixed code ran' from 'broken code failed quietly'."
  ]

  @bugs_found [
    "initiateTrace got 3 args for 4 params (hackService, evidenceLevel>80 branch). initiateTrace catches its own errors and returns {success:false}, so: initiatedBy=serverId, serverId=a NUMBER, evidenceLevel=undefined, Prisma rejected the row — and the next line pushed 'trace_active' unconditionally. Every critical-evidence hack claimed a trace that did not exist, leaving trace.evade (R4) with nothing to evade. Fixed to pass targetUserId (the server owner), matching the correct call site 700 lines earlier which passes session.targetOwnerId."
    "executor.executeSingle() has NEVER existed on ArchitectInterventionExecutor — the methods are execute() and executeBatch(). The TypeError was eaten by a safeExecute fallback, so every epoch-scheduled Architect intervention silently reported 'failed' and none ever ran."
    "progress.totalXP is not a column — the field is `experience` — hidden behind `as any`. xp was always 0, every member scored level 1, estimateFactionPlayerLevel returned 1 for EVERY faction. Faction mission difficulty has been calibrated against a level-1 playerbase since the line was written. The inline sqrt formula was a character-for-character duplicate of the canonical levelForExperience, so it now calls that and the two cannot drift."
    "NOT a bug: forum.factionId narrowing does not survive into an async closure (TS cannot prove the property is unchanged when the callback runs). Bound to a local."
  ]

  @too_narrow_declarations [
    "Two declarations were narrower than their implementations, which is WHY callers reached for `any`: generateUniqueIP takes the IPZone enum (a bare string is a runtime 'Invalid IP zone'), and CreateServerData.ownerId is really string|null (the impl already did `data.ownerId ?? null`; resolveNpcOwnerId returns null)."
    "Lesson: an `any` at a call site is often a symptom of a lying signature upstream, not laziness at the call site."
  ]
}


@phase5_r4_traces {
  @summary [
    "4 defects. R5 is what made them FINDABLE — until the arity fix, no trace was ever created from the counter-measures path, so none of this was observable."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-phase5-r4-traces.ts 12/12, negative-controlled (restoring the bug turns 7 of 12 red). Full suite 18 harnesses / 209 checks / 0 fail."
  ]

  @bugs_fixed [
    "COMPLETION WAS UNREACHABLE. progress = elapsed/totalDuration, so progress>=100 is true at EXACTLY the instant now>=expiresAt — and an expiry check above it continue'd first. Every trace ever ended 'expired'; trace:completed had never fired. Reaching full duration now COMPLETES."
    "Why completion (not expiry) is the right reading: duration SHRINKS as evidence rises, and getTraceEvasionChance FALLS as progress climbs, so evasion must happen early. A trace running to term = the hacker caught. Nothing but evasion was ever going to stop it, so there is no 'ran out of time' outcome to model."
    "THE RESOURCE DRAIN LEAKED. active_trace is a passive consumer (cpu 15 / ram 16 / bw 5). unregisterActiveTrace had ZERO callers in the whole codebase — a player traced once carried the drain for the life of the process."
    "THE DRAIN WAS KEYED WRONG, which would have defeated the leak fix on its own: registerActiveTrace(userId, traceId, label) was passed serverId. Both strings so nothing complained; consumer keyed trace:<serverId> while unregister looks up trace:<traceId>. Same invisible-wrong-argument family as R5's initiateTrace."
    "trace.evade now MATTERS: it releases the drain. Previously it changed a status column and nothing else."
  ]

  @reported_not_fixed [
    "NOTHING listens to trace:completed / trace:evaded / trace:initiated — server or client. Completion logs 'hacker identity exposed', emits, and the event goes nowhere. Giving a completed trace a real consequence is a gameplay decision, not a bug fix."
    "gameBalance.getTraceDuration is DEAD CODE (zero callers; live _getDuration takes evidence only, ignores stealth). Before wiring it up: its `baseMins - stealth*0.3` makes a STEALTHIER player get traced FASTER, which is backwards now that reaching term means being caught."
  ]

  @harness_lesson [
    "The negative control caught a flaw in MY OWN harness, not just the code: R4-d shared a player with R4-b/c, so R4-b/c's leaked consumer made R4-d fail for a reason that had nothing to do with evasion. A cascading failure is a misleading failure — give each block its own fixture."
    "Discriminating assertions matter here specifically: a trace ending 'expired' and one ending 'completed' BOTH end, so 'the trace is no longer active' would have passed before the fix. Every check names the terminal state."
  ]
}


@phase5_p5new_discovery {
  @summary [
    "The PLAN ENTRY WAS WRONG on every specific, and reading the code is the only reason the right thing got fixed. Trusting it would have 'fixed' a function that did not have the bug."
    "Entry claimed: discoverServers is reachable only from handleSubnetSweep's dead fallback, so server:discovered never arrives."
    "Source says: handleSubnetSweep never calls discoverServers in EITHER branch (both call scanByPartialIp); discoverServers has one caller, legacyScan, in the ADJACENCY scan; and the server:discovered emit was already moved onto scanByPartialIp in a75383d, which the entry predates."
    "Verified end-to-end: scan 198.51.100 delivers server:discovered {count:35} to a real connected client."
  ]

  @what_was_actually_broken [
    "spawnBackgroundProcess returns null ONLY when memoryService is absent, and MEMORY_SERVICE is registered unconditionally — so the background branch always returns first and the synchronous branch below it is DEAD."
    "That dead synchronous branch is the one holding `if (results.length === 0) return legacyScan(...)`. So the fallback from 'topology found nothing' to legacy subnet discovery existed in source and could never run: a player on a server with no topology links got 'No unknown servers found' and nothing else, and discoverServers + server:discovered were dead code."
    "Fixed by splitting legacyScan into legacyScan (CommandResult) + legacyScanOutput (string), and calling the latter from the background onComplete when adjacency is empty."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-phase5-p5new-discovery.ts 11/11, negative-controlled."
  ]

  @harness_lessons [
    "TWO harness bugs, both the same species: asserting the state I could SEE instead of the state the CODE READS."
    "(1) Deleted serverLink rows and asserted the DB was empty — but getAdjacentServers reads through a 30s per-server cache (ADJ_CACHE_TTL = 30), so the service still saw a neighbour."
    "(2) After waiting the cache out it STILL saw one, because gameStateManager.createSession calls createHomeLink as a 'fallback if registration missed it' — AUTHENTICATING RE-CREATED THE LINKS THE TEST HAD JUST DELETED. The system self-heals the exact state the test constructs, so the strip must happen AFTER the healing step (authenticate -> strip -> wait cache -> scan)."
    "The server DEBUG log ('discoverNeighbors returned results {count:1}') is what exposed both. When a behavioural test fails, read what the server saw before touching the code."
  ]

  @all_ten_now_fixed [
    "The six that remained after the first pass were fixed 2026-09-25. Full suite 46 harnesses / 0 fail; event-subscriptions 64, state-push 48, notification-persistence 37, balance-constants 33."
    "tap access: gated on DiscoveredLink (servers) and on discovering the target's home server (players); factions ungated as public entities. The EXISTENCE ORACLE went with it — 'no such target' and 'you have not found it' are now one identical message, because tap.remove costs nothing and two errors would let anyone sweep IPs without scanning."
    "game:event:public: publicMetadata() drops any key ending in `id`. Blunt on purpose — an allowlist needs updating whenever a producer adds a field and forgetting fails SILENTLY, while a missing field gets noticed."
    "Tap consumption: stock test moved into the WHERE clause (the spendCredits idiom), routed through shopService.removeItemFromInventory so item:removed fires, consumes BEFORE subscribing, and REFUNDS on a cap rejection with a loud log if the refund itself fails."
    "createSubscription: database writes first, in-memory map last. A failed insert used to leave a live unexpired unrecorded tap while the command reported failure and kept the item."
    "mirrorPlayerState no longer mirrors currentServer — applyStateDelta copies only along the delta path, so state.currentServer stays frozen at login and re-setting it reverted the player to their login server after any credit change."
    "Notification retention: purgeOldNotifications (30d for read/dismissed, plus anything past expiry) on a 6h registerShutdownTimer. UNREAD ROWS ARE NEVER TOUCHED at any age — a purge that could delete an unseen security warning would reintroduce the bug persistence exists to fix."
    "Comment cluster: server:discovered double-notify removed, showNotification JSDoc reattached to its method (it had bound to lastResyncAt), and the tap catalog `effect` block documented as a MIRROR of TAP_ITEMS with verify-balance-constants asserting the copies agree."
  ]

  @two_harness_checks_were_themselves_wrong [
    "verify-event-subscriptions asserted 'subscribes BEFORE consuming' — it had encoded the OLD design. Subscribe-first protected the item from a cap rejection but left the consume racy; consume-first-with-refund is strictly better, so the check now asserts that instead."
    "verify-phase5-o9-timers required EXACTLY TWO registered inline timers and failed the moment a correct third was added. Now asserts the invariant — every inline setInterval in index.ts is registered — which cannot rot as the count changes. The 'assume every number is stale' rule biting a HARNESS rather than a doc."
    "And the first concurrency check was STRUCTURAL: it asserted which METHOD was called, so restoring the race tripped nothing. Rewritten to fire five concurrent consumes against one item and assert exactly one wins — it now goes red (2 of 5 succeeded) against the old code."
  ]

  @fixed_findings_were_listed_here_as_open [
    "server:alert, server:created, server:deleted, server:updated, server:disconnected have NO client listener at all — 5 of serverService's 6 event names emit into the void. Unlike server:discovered nothing is waiting for them, so wiring them is a feature decision, not a repair."
    "Legacy subnet discovery from a PLAYER HOME is near-useless: homes get random 10.x.y.z and each is essentially alone in its /16, so discoverServers scoped to that /16 finds nothing. The fallback only helps on a non-home server in a populated subnet."
  ]
}


@phase5_r6_session_binding {
  @summary [
    "Checked all 3 plan claims against source FIRST. Two held; one was a trap that would have caused a regression."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-phase5-r6-session.ts 4/4, negative-controlled (reverting turns exactly the 2 R6 assertions red, positive controls stay green)."
  ]

  @claim1_was_a_trap [
    "PLAN SAID: 'call the socket-aware handleDisconnect(socketId)'. READING IT: the method only resolved userId from activeConnections and called destroySession(userId) — exactly what the socket layer already does, MINUS the isLastSocket guard added in Phase 4."
    "So following the plan would have destroyed a session the user's other tabs were still using — re-introducing the bug Phase 4 had just fixed. DELETED the method instead: a zero-caller method whose name suggests it is the right thing to call is a trap for the next reader."
  ]

  @claims_2_and_3_confirmed [
    "session.socketId was written ONCE in createSession and never again. handleAuthentication's reuse branch joined only user:/player:, never server:<currentServerId>. Both come from the same three lines: if a session already existed, authentication bound NOTHING."
    "PHASE 4 IS WHAT MADE THIS LIVE: sessions now survive while the user holds other sockets, so session.socketId can name a CLOSED socket while the player keeps playing. Both room ops did io.sockets.sockets.get(deadId) -> undefined -> silently nothing. Closing the tab that created the session left the player on a server receiving none of its broadcasts."
  ]

  @fixes [
    "Room join/leave now use io.in(`user:<id>`).socketsJoin/socketsLeave — covers ALL of a user's sockets, no longer depends on which socket is 'the' socket. A single-socket leave would equally have left other tabs in the room of a server they had left."
    "attachSocket(userId, socketId): rebinds socketId, fixes activeConnections, returns the session so the caller can rejoin server:<id>."
    "detachSocket(userId, socketId, survivingSocketId?): removes the CLOSING socket (destroySession deleted activeConnections[session.socketId] — the wrong key whenever the closing socket was not the bound one, leaking an entry per extra socket) and hands the binding to a survivor."
  ]

  @harness_lesson [
    "First draft asserted on server:user_disconnected, having ASSUMED the room broadcast preceded the room removal. Source says the opposite: socketsLeave runs BEFORE its broadcast, so the leaving player's own sockets are already out of the room. Rebuilt on server:user_connected, where socketsJoin verifiably precedes the broadcast and io.to(room) does not exclude the sender."
    "Room membership is NOT observable from a client — the only way to test it is to emit into the room and assert delivery, paired with a positive control (a socket that MUST receive), since 'did not receive' is also what a broken emit looks like."
  ]
}


@phase5_r7_timelimit_and_rewards {
  @summary [
    "Two halves. Units: verified + fixed. Multipliers: verified, then DECIDED WITH THE MAINTAINER because it changes balance, not correctness."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-phase5-r7-timelimit.ts 17/17, negative-controlled. Full suite 21 harnesses / 250 checks / 0 fail."
  ]

  @timelimit_units [
    "WRITTEN IN TWO UNITS, READ IN A THIRD ASSUMPTION. Producers: missionGenerator template path = seconds*1000 (ms); missionGenerator AI path = difficulty*3600*1000 (ms); personaMissionGenService = raw template (seconds); storyMissionService = raw template (seconds). All THREE readers multiplied by 1000 (assumed seconds)."
    "Effect: the two ms producers gave expiries 1000x too long — a template documenting '1–2 hours' expired in 41–83 days. MEASURED: 187 of 187 missions with a timeLimit held a ms value. Mission expiry was disabled game-wide."
    "Chose SECONDS, against the plan's suggested ms: every human-authored source already uses seconds (templates comment 3600–7200 as '1–2 hours'; gameBalance minigame limits are seconds, UI prints 's'), and all three readers already expected seconds — so the fix lands on 2 producers instead of 3 readers. utils/missionTime.ts is the only place a timeLimit is multiplied or divided."
    "Existing rows still hold ms and stay wrong until the db:reset (task #5)."
  ]

  @rewards_that_never_varied [
    "PARTLY OVERTAKEN BY R7 — corrected 2026-09-24. Only the STEALTH half became the flat constant: BASELINE_COMPLETION_BONUS = 0.2 (gameBalance.ts:253), because detectionCount is read in completeMission and written nowhere, so stealthScore was always 100. The EFFICIENCY half (+0.15) is now a REAL lever, not pretence: it is scored over REQUIRED objectives only, since bonus objectives became genuinely optional when isBonus was made a real column. Full completion still pays +0.35, but skipping bonus objectives now earns less than it used to. missionService.ts:1096-1114, :1275-1299."
    "bonusObjectivesCompleted was max(0, completed - total) — MATHEMATICALLY always 0, since completed counts a subset of total."
    "isBonus was declared on templates, set on 39 objectives, documented 'failure doesn't fail the mission' — then DROPPED during generation, with no column on PlayerMissionObjective to live in. Completion was objectives.every(completed) with no exclusion, so bonus objectives were mandatory, which is WHY the bonus count could never be positive."
    "Now: carried through both generators, persisted in a new is_bonus column, restored on read, and requiredObjectivesComplete ignores bonus objectives — falling back to requiring ALL if a mission is somehow all-bonus ('no required objectives' must not mean 'complete immediately')."
  ]

  @lessons [
    "A fix can be correct at the source and inert at a PERSISTENCE BOUNDARY. isBonus reached Mission.objectives (Json) but PlayerMissionObjective is typed COLUMNS — no column meant the flag was silently discarded and requiredObjectivesComplete would never have seen it. The round-trip check is what caught it; the generator-level check passed happily."
    "`prisma generate` rewrites node_modules/@prisma/client, which tsx watch does NOT watch. After adding a column the long-running dev server kept a STALE client and two unrelated harnesses failed with 'Unknown argument isBonus' — a stale-process failure that reads exactly like a code regression. Bounce the watch server after every prisma generate."
    "Reported a check that never ran: `cd server && npx tsc` where cwd was ALREADY server — cd failed, tsc never executed, and the following `echo TSC_DONE` printed anyway. Verify the command ran, not just that the script reached the echo."
  ]
}

@orphan_audit_2026_09_24 {
  @summary [
    "Audit of unreachable code across five dimensions (methods, socket events, DB models, constants, client APIs), then a wiring pass."
    "THE FRAMING THAT MATTERED (maintainer's call): the question is not 'does this have callers' but 'is this functionality we LOSE by deleting it'. Zero callers answers whether something is REACHABLE, never whether it is WANTED. Reordering the audit around that changed the disposition of most items from delete to wire."
    "Result so far: 7 items wired, 2 correctly REFUSED after reading the code, 2 filed to Phase 8. The two refusals are the load-bearing part of this section — both looked like obvious wins."
  ]

  @refused_after_reading [
    "IP discovery / traceroute / range-scan cluster — these are PREDECESSORS, not missing features. They hop randomly; the live path uses topoService.findPath against the real topology. Wiring them would have REPLACED a correct implementation with a fake one. Deleted instead."
    "getTraceDuration — INVERTED. Higher stealth produced a SHORTER trace, i.e. more stealth got you caught sooner. Wiring it would have shipped a backwards mechanic behind a green structural check. Left unwired and documented."
    "Both were caught by reading the implementation, not by the audit, which scored them as clean wins."
  ]

  @wired [
    "Faction wars — declareWar/surrender/score/multiplier were unreachable TOGETHER, which is why the feature was invisible rather than buggy. Wired via the declare_war Architect intervention (the third parameter is documented `declaredBy // AI persona ID`: wars were designed as AI world events) and getReputationMultiplier into reputationEngine.applyReputationChange, the single chokepoint. KNOWN GAP: nothing calls updateWarScore, so a war sits 0-0 and the monitor resolves on time, not merit."
    "Hack cooldown — hacking skill now reduces it (applyCooldown became async and looks the skill up itself)."
    "MAX_ACTIVE_MISSIONS — enforced in acceptMission, before the mutate so a refusal does not consume the offer."
    "Socket group B — security:warning, reputation:changed, force:disconnect, connection:refused, terminal:error, moderation:flagged. Each was the player's only signal for a honeypot, a rep change, a kick, a refused connection or a failed tab op."
    "Progress backup — createBackup/createBackupForAll/deleteOldBackups/restoreBackup all had ZERO callers and ProgressBackup had 0 rows, so there was NO recovery path for player progress at all. Now on an hourly interval, reentrancy-guarded, cleared on stop()."
    "Notification persistence — see @notification_persistence."
    "Event subscriptions + the whole event-delivery layer — see @event_delivery."
    "state:update / state:delta — the authoritative state push. See @state_push."
  ]

  @notification_persistence [
    "The Notification model was fully specced (5 indexes, read/dismiss/expiry columns, a User back-relation) and NEVER written or read. Delivery was socket-only, the client store in-memory, so a refresh discarded every unseen alert including security warnings."
    "utils/notify.ts is the single write point; 6 per-user emit sites (missionService x4, hackService x2) route through it. sockets/handlers.ts replays unseen rows on authenticate."
    "Ordering is load-bearing: markNotificationsRead runs AFTER the emit, so a failed emit leaves them pending rather than destroying them. Conversely, a persistence failure does NOT block delivery — a database blip must not swallow a security warning."
    "epochSchedulerService's world-event announcement deliberately stays transient: it is a global io.emit, and persisting it would mean one row per user for an ephemeral broadcast."
    "THREE CLIENT BUGS THAT ONLY THE ROUND TRIP EXPOSED. Writing rows is the easy half. add() stamped timestamp: new Date() unconditionally, so a three-hour-old alert displayed as if it had just fired; it played a sound per notification, so a 50-item backlog meant 50 overlapping tones; and the socket listener RE-DERIVED priority from severity, discarding the server's value so everything not exactly critical became high. add() now also dedupes on the server row id, because replay can race a live emit of the same row."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-notification-persistence.ts 30/30. Negative-controlled two ways — reverting to emit-only (7 red) and moving markNotificationsRead before the emit (ordering check red)."
  ]

  @lessons [
    "'Zero callers' answers IS IT REACHABLE, never IS IT WANTED. Two of eight candidates were superseded or inverted; deleting or wiring either on the caller count alone would have been wrong."
    "A wiring job is not done when the write lands. Persistence with no reader is the same dead feature in a new place — and the read side is where both the progress-backup bugs and all three notification bugs lived."
    "The priority bug is the R13 shape one level up: a correct value computed server-side and thrown away by the consumer. Fixing the producer does not fix the pipeline."
    "A negative control found a VACUOUS assertion: comparing the emitted id to the row id passed as undefined === undefined when nothing persisted."
  ]

  @backlog_note [
    "THIS FILE IS BEHIND. The last phase section is @phase5_r7_timelimit_and_rewards; Phase 5 R9-R13, all of Phase 6 and all of Phase 7 (A2/A3/A5, characterization harness) are recorded in PLAN.md and ORPHAN_AUDIT.md but NOT here. Treat the absence of a section as 'not yet written', not 'not done'."
  ]
}

@event_delivery {
  @summary [
    "The orphan audit filed this as 'event subscriptions are dead on both ends'. Reading the code found something larger: NO GameEvent had ever reached a player. 34 rows in the dev database, zero deliveries, broken in two opposite directions at once."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-event-subscriptions.ts 45/45, negative-controlled four ways. Full suite 45 harnesses / 0 fail. Golden master re-recorded (help's network count 6/7 -> 8/9, the only diff)."
  ]

  @the_two_broken_halves [
    "PERSISTED BUT NEVER BROADCAST: hackService.sendSecurityAlert and both fileService honeypot blocks called prisma.gameEvent.create DIRECTLY, skipping createEvent and therefore broadcastEvent. 30 of the 34 rows are theirs. fileService has NO emit of any kind, so for a honeypot that row was the owner's only signal."
    "BROADCAST BUT TO NOBODY: createEvent (storyProgression weight>=7) and all four createSystemAlert callers are isGlobal with affectedUsers: []. The global branch of getEventRecipients was a stub whose body added exactly the affectedUsers the next line already added — comment: 'Would query for online users, for now just affected users'. Empty in, empty out."
    "NO ACQUISITION PATH for the only other route to becoming a recipient: no /api/events route is mounted (the server has four route files — admin, adminApi, auth, command), no socket handler, no command, no item. loadSubscriptionsFromDatabase ran at startup against a table nothing could write."
    "AND THE CLIENT DROPPED WHAT ARRIVED: two game:event listeners on one name with incompatible payload shapes; a gameEvents store with zero consumers; an empty game:event:public stub; state.notifications (built from getUserEvents on EVERY state broadcast) dying at state:update, which has no listener; and showNotification — 12 call sites — was an EMPTY METHOD, 7 of them with no other notification."
  ]

  @fixes [
    "Delivery split on the same rule notifyUser set: targeted -> notifyUser (persists, replays), global -> one io.emit (transient)."
    "THE PUBLIC EMIT IS NOW GATED ON isGlobal. It used to fire for every event including targeted ones. Ordering mattered: routing the honeypot through createEvent would have INTRODUCED that leak if the gate had not gone in first."
    "Both honeypot writers go through createEvent via one alertHoneypot helper. EVENT_SERVICE resolved lazily — a static import of the container closes a require cycle."
    "hackService.sendSecurityAlert DELIBERATELY keeps its direct write: its caller calls it an audit log and notifyServerOwner (3 sites) is the player channel. Routing both would double-notify. Commented in place so it is not 'fixed' later."
    "showNotification is real; the 5 story sites that already called ns.add had their redundant call removed."
    "game:event retired in both directions — one channel, one shape, one listener."
    "HONEYPOT_TRIGGERED added to EventType: fileService wrote it as a raw string, so it was unsubscribable BY CONSTRUCTION."
  ]

  @the_tap [
    "Acquisition path chosen by the maintainer: a shop item. basic_tap 1200c/q30/60m, shielded_tap 4500c/q60/3h, quantum_tap 18000c/q90/12h. Uses `effect` (the payload tokenConsumption reads), NOT `effects` (the stat bag nothing applies) — a tap there would sell, install and do nothing."
    "`tap <ip|player|faction>` is ONE command for three target kinds because targetId already matched against metadata.serverId || factionId || targetUserId. The command takes advantage of that rather than adding anything."
    "TWO OF THE FOUR SUBSCRIPTION FIELDS WERE INERT. `quality` was documented 'affects event detail/reliability' and did neither — the only code reading it fired for INFO below 30, so 30 and 100 behaved identically and detail was never varied anywhere. Now a real dial across every severity (utils/eventIntercept.ts), with CRITICAL deterministic at every tier so a breach is never silently dropped. `method` is STILL never read — left as recorded provenance rather than five invented behaviours."
    "Three further bugs found while wiring: quality persisted UNCLAMPED while the in-memory copy was clamped (a restart resurrected an uncapped tap); re-tapping the same target inserted another isActive row every time, invisible because the Map collapses them at startup; and no cap existed while getEventRecipients walks the map on every event (now MAX_ACTIVE_TAPS = 5)."
  ]

  @lessons [
    "'Dead on both ends' can still understate it. The audit counted callers; the failure was that BOTH halves of a live pipeline were broken in opposite directions, which no caller count can show."
    "Check for a competing channel before routing anything. sendSecurityAlert and the honeypots look identical — both raw gameEvent.create — and the correct action was opposite for each, because one had a sibling notifier and the other had none."
    "Order the fixes so you do not introduce the leak you are about to widen. The isGlobal gate had to land before the honeypot was routed through the same function."
    "A fixture that fails twice before passing is worth more than one that passes first try. 'File not found' then 'Permission denied' are the two ways this honeypot test could have been silently vacuous."
  ]
}

@state_push {
  @summary [
    "state:update and state:delta were emitted with ZERO client listeners, and broadcastStateDelta had ZERO CALLERS — dead at both ends. Every authenticate ran a five-way parallel query batch and discarded it."
    "Maintainer chose BUILD IT OUT over delete. Evidence: scripts/verify-state-push.ts 36/36, negative-controlled three ways. Full suite 46 harnesses / 0 fail, golden master unchanged."
  ]

  @three_dead_layers [
    "PUSH: no listeners, and no update mechanism either — broadcastStateDelta had zero callers, so the push was a login-time SNAPSHOT. That detail is what settled the disposition: wiring the UI to it as-is would have frozen credits at login, strictly worse than the status quo."
    "REST FALLBACK: loadInitialGameData ran on login, register AND token-verify, calling /api/users/stats and /api/servers. Both 404 — CONFIRMED BY CURL against the running dev server; only /api/csrf-token, /api/admin, /api/command, /api/commands and /api/auth are mounted. Both sat behind their own try/catch returning {} / [], so the failure was completely silent — and because knownServers was therefore always empty, the connectToServerInternal(homeServer) branch under it could never run either."
    "STORES: playerProgress, knownServers, currentServer, discoveryLevel, currentDirectory, connectedServers have ZERO consumers. Terminal.svelte's currentServer is a local `let`, not the store. Only currentUser is imported anywhere."
    "The live path is command-driven: ShopDialog got credits by running the `status` COMMAND, re-run on every open via `$: if (visible) loadPlayerData()` — a workaround for having no push channel."
  ]

  @how_it_was_built [
    "applyStateDelta lives in shared/utils/stateDelta.ts, NOT the client. A delta channel only works if both sides agree what a path means, and that agreement is the fragile part."
    "Deltas originate at PlayerProgressRepository — the single sanctioned writer of player_progress — so ONE subscription covers hack rewards, mission payouts, trace penalties and command grants, instead of 23 call sites. The repository's docstring says it 'deliberately does no I/O beyond the database', so it emits an IN-PROCESS event and gameStateManager (which owns io) does the socket work. The rule is preserved, not bent."
    "shopService's five orphan events (item:added, item:removed, purchase:complete, item:sold, item:used) finally have a subscriber — it already extends EventEmitter and the emits were already in the right places, they just went nowhere. Inventory changes trigger a ONE-query slice refresh vs the five-query full state."
    "Resync on drift: an unappliable delta means the sides disagree, so the client asks for a full state. Throttled one-per-5s client-side AND rate limited server-side, because the trigger repeats for every subsequent delta."
    "TRANSACTION HAZARD: addCredits/spendCredits end with a read through the same tx, so inside a transaction they observe UNCOMMITTED state. Announcing it would push a balance that can still roll back. Suppressed under tx; transactional callers (shopService.purchaseItem) emit their own post-commit event which the bridge picks up."
    "KNOWN GAP kept open and stated: the nine direct player_progress writers produce no delta. Four are row creation (nothing to send); the two that matter are the admin progress editor and restoreBackup, both absolute writes. Missions have no delta yet."
  ]

  @lessons [
    "'APPLIED' IS NOT 'CORRECT'. The negative control renamed the server path from player.skills.hacking to player.skill_hacking — it still walked `player` and set a leaf, so every delta reported APPLIED while the value landed where nothing reads. client=10, db=17, no error anywhere. Only comparing the applied result against the DATABASE catches it. Any future delta/patch channel needs that comparison, not an applied-count."
    "SVELTE-CHECK DOES NOT CATCH A DELETED FUNCTION STILL BEING CALLED. Removing loadPlayerData left a live call in ShopDialog's purchase handler and svelte-check reported 0 errors; a real `vite build` is now part of the routine."
    "The disposition hinged on FREQUENCY, not reachability. Caller counts said 'dead, delete it'; what actually mattered was that the one caller fired only at login, which is what made naive wiring worse than the bug."
    "Look for an existing in-process bus before adding call sites. shopService's five emits had been sitting in exactly the right places with no subscriber — the bridge was a subscription, not new instrumentation."
  ]
}

@code_review_2026_09_25 {
  @summary [
    "8-angle review over the whole uncommitted orphan-wiring diff (1849 lines, 22 files). 48 raw candidates, 10 after dedup and verification. 4 fixed, 6 filed."
    "THE HEADLINE: the harnesses stood at 36/36 and 45/45 while ALL TEN findings were true."
  ]

  @how_my_own_harnesses_lied [
    "SP-4 proved the TRANSACTION GUARD suppresses an announcement, and stopped. It never asked whether a purchase still updates credits — it does not, because both money paths run under tx and the bridge only refreshed inventory. FIVE OF EIGHT review angles found this independently; the harness found none of it."
    "Only a SERVER tap was ever tested. Player and faction taps could never match: the target check short-circuited on `metadata.serverId || factionId || targetUserId` and nearly every event carries a serverId."
    "Structural checks confirmed ShopDialog SUBSCRIBES to the store. Nothing checked the store ever CHANGES."
  ]

  @fixed [
    "Credits delta on purchase/sale — the bridge now refreshes credits as well as inventory from the post-commit shop event, coalesced per player (one useItem emits two events, a purchase emits two). This was a straight REGRESSION: ShopDialog's re-poll was deleted citing a comment of mine that was false, repeated in the repository docstring."
    "Tap targeting — the matcher compares against every candidate instead of the first present. attackerId is deliberately NOT a candidate: a tap watches what happens TO a target, not what it does to others."
    "`use <tap>` destroyed the item and did nothing — useItem deletes any isConsumable and returns success, never reading `effect`. Now refuses and names the tap command."
    "Notification replay marked rows read on an UNCONFIRMED emit. socket.emit neither throws nor confirms delivery, so the guard the comment described could never fire. Now ack-gated with a timeout, emits OLDEST-FIRST (query is newest-first and the client prepends, so the backlog rendered upside down and the newest were evicted at the 50 cap), and carries the persisted `category` — which had been written to the row and dropped from every payload, making the client's `data.category === \"security\"` branch unreachable."
    "Folded in: the inventory shape mismatch. The delta pushed {itemId, item, quantity} while state:update pushed {id, name, type,...} at the same path. One `toStateInventory` now serves both — two producers for one path was the bug."
  ]

  @still_open [
    "`tap` has no discovery or ownership check — any player can tap any home server by IP and read its honeypot alerts including the raw attackerId. This is WHY the targeting fix stopped at the matcher and did not widen producer metadata to carry player ids: extending reach before the access check would make it worse."
    "`game:event:public` now carries event.metadata, which includes raw User.ids — a leak I introduced by widening the payload, in a game that sells 10000-credit aliases."
    "Tap consumption is a non-transactional check-then-act (N taps for one item) and bypasses shopService, so it is the one inventory mutation emitting no delta."
    "createSubscription mutates the in-memory map before its two DB writes; mirrorPlayerState re-sets currentServer on every delta; Notification rows grow unbounded (nothing sets expiresAt, nothing deletes)."
    "Comment cluster: `effect: {tapQuality}` on the catalog rows is read by NOTHING (TAP_ITEMS in gameBalance is the live copy) — a fresh bug shape #6 with a comment asserting the opposite; server:discovered now double-notifies; two JSDoc blocks bind to the wrong member."
  ]

  @lessons [
    "NEGATIVE-CONTROL THE OUTCOME, NOT THE MECHANISM. Every control I wrote flipped the switch I had just installed and confirmed the switch moved. None asked whether the light came on. Phrase the assertion as 'the client's credit value changed' and 'the item survives', never 'the guard fired'."
    "Test every branch of a feature's OWN design. Three target kinds were chosen; one was tested; two were broken."
    "A wiring change can regress the thing it replaces. Deleting a poll is only safe once the push is observed end to end — and the justification for deleting it was a comment, which is the weakest possible evidence in this repo."
    "My gap-check grepped an identifier across a whole file and fired on unrelated function parameters. A non-empty grep is not a finding: assert on the metadata literal, not the word."
  ]
}

@finishing_the_orphan_work {
  @summary [
    "Four decisions taken BEFORE committing, to avoid leaving the wiring half done. Full suite 46 harnesses / 0 fail; event-subscriptions 70, state-push 56. Golden master unchanged."
  ]

  @all_three_tap_targets_are_real [
    "Checking every live createEvent/createSystemAlert caller showed NO producer emitted a factionId or a victim's user id — so after the matcher fix, player and faction taps were still PURCHASABLE NO-OPS. A player could spend 18000 credits and receive nothing, silently."
    "alertHoneypot now carries targetUserId; the declare_war intervention raises createFactionWarEvent — reviving a dead factory AND giving faction taps their only possible match at once."
    "THAT REQUIRED SOLVING A LEAK FIRST. broadcastEvent splits NAMED RECIPIENTS from WATCHERS: the party an event names gets the full record, a watcher gets the same news with identifiers scrubbed by the same publicMetadata the global channel uses. A tapper learns a honeypot fired on a server they discovered and does NOT learn who tripped it — a tap must not be a cheaper deanonymiser than the 10000-credit alias reveal."
  ]

  @missions_are_the_fifth_slice [
    "PlayerMissionRepository gained the same in-process announcer as the progress repository. Emitted from writeMission — the single private write path behind put/mutate/mutateAll, so it CANNOT fire for a skipped write (mutate returns early on NO_CHANGE, mutateAll unless the callback returns true) — and from incrementObjective, which uses raw SQL and never reaches writeMission."
    "One subscription covers missionService, tutorialService, storyMissionService and missionGenerator alike."
    "toStateMissions joins toStateInventory as ONE definition per path — the inventory shape mismatch was caused by two producers for one path."
  ]

  @delta_channel_and_debounce [
    "broadcastStateDelta takes an `operation`, so push/remove/update are reachable from a producer for the first time instead of being implemented, declared in the wire contract, and dead."
    "The debounce generalised with it: one scheduleSliceRefresh(userId, ...slices) with a pending SET per player, so a burst collapses into one pass. The `slice` parameter review called speculative generality is a real union now — inventory | credits | missions."
  ]

  @lesson [
    "A HARNESS CHECK THAT ENCODES A LIMITATION EXPIRES WHEN THE LIMITATION DOES. verify-event-subscriptions asserted 'no live producer keys an event to a player' — a true statement about a gap, written as a check. Closing the gap turned it RED. Rewritten to assert the positive plus the owner-sees-ids / tapper-does-not split. That is a second way for a green suite to mislead, alongside testing the mechanism instead of the outcome."
    "Ask what is HALF DONE before committing, not after. Three of the four decisions here were features that looked finished and were inert for one of their cases — the tap in two of three target kinds, the state push in two of five slices, the delta channel in three of four operations."
  ]
}

@audit_closed_2026_09_25 {
  @summary [
    "Every remaining orphan-audit item is now wired, refused with a reason, or filed to Phase 8 with one. Full suite 46 harnesses / 0 fail; golden master re-recorded once (see below)."
  ]

  @wired [
    "Four orphaned socket events got listeners: faction:contest_started/resolved (the payoff of the whole contest system produced no on-screen event), server:alert (the ATTACKER branch had no client equivalent, so a trace could start with nothing on screen), story:fragment-intel (tells you who holds the fragment you need — composed, addressed correctly, dropped)."
    "THE FORUM ROOM NOBODY JOINS. forum:new-post/new-reply go to `forum:<forumId>` and nothing had ever joined it — the subtlest find in the audit, because a NAME-COMPARISON audit scores those events healthy: the server emits them and the client listens for them. Rooms are joined on authenticate from ForumMember, and on registration via io.in(user:<id>).socketsJoin so a mid-session registration reaches every open tab."
    "updateWarScore has a producer at last: warfareService.recordHackForWar, called from the post-hack pipeline on success only. The war lookup lives in warfareService because that service owns what a war IS; hackService only knows a hack succeeded. Every declared war previously sat 0-0 and its monitor resolved on elapsed time rather than on merit."
    "Passive drain runs: registerConnection/registerBackdoor had zero callers so PASSIVE_COSTS, addPassiveConsumer and the resource ticker all ran against a permanently empty set. THE GOLDEN MASTER CAUGHT THIS as a real behaviour change and the diff was exactly the intended effect — a [connection] consumer appearing in `ps` with RAM/BW totals moving. Better evidence than any structural check."
    "rejectDraft had zero callers because the admin route RE-IMPLEMENTED it inline against raw Prisma while the approve route two blocks up used the service. Two implementations of one transition, only one of which logged. The route now delegates."
    "Review leftovers: notifyUser's envelope now wins over producer data (and emits `category` on the LIVE path, not only the replay); GameStateManager gained a public stop() and a seat in the shutdown table. CORRECTED 2026-10-06: this entry used to say authenticated/authenticate:request are rate-limited. THEY ARE NOT — the limit was added here and REVERTED in review #3, because generalRateLimit is the shared per-user budget typing indicators also spend, so exhausting it and reconnecting produced a live socket in no rooms with no state. See @review3_2026_10_06."
    "The client's REST connect path is deleted — /api/servers/:id/connect and /disconnect are not mounted (404, curled) and no component called them. Connecting is `connect <ip>` over /api/command."
  ]

  @deliberately_open [
    "#2 hack alerts and #15 handleNPCReply — Phase 8, unchanged."
    "#10 getTraceDuration — still REFUSED. It is INVERTED: more stealth gets you caught sooner."
    "Backdoor drains are not restored at boot: the consumer map is rebuilt empty on restart, so a backdoor never re-installed or upgraded stays unregistered. Restoring from the Backdoor table at startup is its own change."
    "The 9 direct player_progress writers still emit no delta. PROJECT_KNOWLEDGE is three phases behind (Phase 5 R9-R13, Phase 6, Phase 7)."
  ]

  @lessons [
    "A GOLDEN MASTER EARNS ITS KEEP ON THE CHANGES YOU THINK ARE INERT. Wiring the passive drain was 'just adding a call' and it moved two commands' output; the diff being exactly the intended effect is what made it safe to re-record rather than a reason to panic."
    "'Zero callers' sometimes means A SECOND IMPLEMENTATION EXISTS, not that the feature is missing. rejectDraft was unreachable because its route had inlined the same logic — the fix was deletion of the duplicate, not new wiring."
    "An event with a matching name on both sides can still be unwired: forum:new-post was emitted and listened for, and went to a room nobody joined. Match the ROOM, not just the name."
  ]
}

@review3_2026_10_06 {
  @summary [
    "Third review over the uncommitted orphan-wiring changeset. 10 findings -> 15 -> 21 across three rounds, roughly half of each round being defects in the previous round's fixes. The trajectory, not the findings, is the result worth recording: iterative fixing was not converging."
  ]

  @why_it_did_not_converge [
    "NEW MACHINERY FOR AN UNDEMONSTRATED RISK. The rate limit on `authenticated`, the ack-gating on notification replay, and the passive-drain wiring each produced a finding in EVERY review they existed for. None guarded a failure anyone had observed. The rate limit is now reverted."
    "COMMENTS ASSERTING FACTS ABOUT OTHER FILES — ~40% of all findings across three rounds. The block in client/src/stores/gameState.ts was wrong three consecutive times, each after an explicit correction, and the version review #3 saw still named connectToServerInternal and disconnectFromServer as live writers of currentServer AFTER the same changeset deleted both functions. ~60 lines of that archaeology are now deleted rather than corrected a fourth time."
  ]

  @fixed [
    "memoryService.releaseAllFor -> releaseSessionConsumersFor. The old one was passiveConsumers.delete(userId), so one logout wiped BACKDOOR drains too — and nothing re-registers those at login, only installBackdoor does. A tab close was a permanent free upgrade. Now releases `terminal` and `connection` only."
    "warfareService.warSweepInterval is armed in the CONSTRUCTOR, so startWarMonitor's teardown never covered it. stopWarMonitor — the only shutdown hook the service has — now clears it, and the callback goes through safeExecute instead of a bare `void`. A sweep firing after prisma.$disconnect() threw from inside a timer callback where nothing was left to catch it."
    "eventService.createSubscription rolled back with activeSubscriptions.delete(subId). Re-tapping the same (user, type, target) REUSES that key, so a failed insert on a re-tap destroyed the working subscription the player already held. Now captures `previous` and restores it."
    "warfareService.recordHackForWar was check-then-act: read warContributed, await updateWarScore, then write. Concurrent hacks all read the same pre-write total and all awarded. Both maps are now claimed BEFORE the await with rollback on failure. Measured: 5 concurrent hacks awarded 400 against a cap of 200."
    "gameStateManager.flushSlices deleted the pending set before awaiting, and refreshStateSlices wraps its body in safeExecute, which swallows — so one transient query failure dropped the slice permanently, with `applied` true on the client so no resync fired. refreshStateSlices now returns a boolean and the slices are put back on failure. Deliberately does NOT re-arm the debounce: retrying every SLICE_REFRESH_DEBOUNCE_MS against a down database is a hot loop. The restored slices ride along with the next event."
    "notify.ts never marked a LIVE delivery read. purgeOldNotifications deliberately never touches unread rows and getPendingNotifications takes 50, so an online player's alerts accumulated forever and the replay backlog was permanent. Room occupancy of player:<id> is now read BEFORE the insert and written as isRead in the same statement — one write, not two, and deterministic for a test."
    "forumService.banMember set isBanned and never evicted the socket, so the ban did nothing to a session already in the room until it reconnected. Now io.in(user:<id>).socketsLeave(forum:<id>)."
    "forumService.getLiveFeedForums streamed requiresProxy forums that accessForum refuses to show without a proxy. Excluded outright rather than proxy-checked: room membership is decided once at authenticate while proxy status changes mid-session, so no answer stays true. Those forums are still readable interactively, where the check applies."
  ]

  @closed_the_four_open [
    "AUTH IS LIMITED AGAIN, ON ITS OWN BUDGET. UserLimiters.auth, 5 per 10s, spent by nothing else. The original error was charging it to `general`, which typing indicators also spend. On a dedicated budget, reaching the limit can only mean the client is looping, so refusing is correct — and the refusal answers the ack rather than leaving a promise Socket.IO never settles."
    "ONE FORUM LIVE-FEED RULE, FOUR CALLERS. ForumService.LIVE_FEED_MEMBERSHIP is the predicate; syncLiveFeedRoom(userId, forumId) joins or leaves to match it. getLiveFeedForums, registerForumAccount, banMember and unbanMember all route through it. Two of the four used to restate the rule and two had none, so a mid-session registration subscribed a player to feeds the authenticate path refused — visible one session, gone the next."
    "unbanMember rejoins the room. That asymmetry was created by the ban fix: an unban left the player outside, able to read and post with a silently dead live feed until reload."
    "LIVE NOTIFICATIONS ARE ACK-GATED, matching the replay path. Room membership was the wrong signal: Socket.IO keeps a socket in its rooms for pingInterval + pingTimeout (~45s) after the connection really dies, so a sleeping laptop had its security alerts written isRead and emitted into the void — the exact failure the replay path had already been fixed to avoid. Now io.to(room).timeout(10s).emit(..., cb), marked read only if at least one client answers. Broadcast acks deliver the responses received so far even on timeout, so it keys off the response array, not the error."
    "removeDetectedBackdoor was a third inlined copy of the drain-release block and now calls releaseBackdoorDrain with the other three."
  ]

  @lessons [
    "COUNT, DO NOT MATCH, WHEN A RULE HAS MORE THAN ONE SITE. Two instances in one sitting. (a) `/authRateLimit\\(\\)/.test(block)` stayed GREEN with the limit removed from `authenticated`, because `authenticate:request` still had one — the control could not distinguish 'both guarded' from 'one guarded', which is the half of the bug that matters. Now it counts guards against handlers and asserts two of each. (b) The backdoor check, written to count from the start, immediately reported `3 release calls for 4 deactivations` and found a path I had not looked at. A matching check would have passed. This is the same defect in my VERIFICATION that review #4 found in my code: single-path reasoning about a multi-path change."
    "A HARNESS CHECK THAT ENCODES A MECHANISM EXPIRES WHEN THE MECHANISM DOES. NP-9 asserted that the rate-limited auth path answered its ack; reverting the rate limit turned it red for being right. Rewritten to assert the OUTCOME (generalRateLimit does not appear in the auth handlers) with a positive control on the slice and a negative control proving the file still rate-limits elsewhere."
    "RED FOR THE WRONG REASON PROVES NOTHING. The first negative control for the war-scoring race used a regex that also ate the enclosing `try {`, so the harness went red on what was effectively a syntax error. Redone as a valid edit moving two statements after the await; exactly one check went red, reporting +400 against a cap of 200."
    "PATCH THE CLIENT THE CODE ACTUALLY READS. The first ES-13 control stubbed prisma.eventSubscription.create on the harness's own `new PrismaClient()`. eventService imports prisma from database/client, so the insert sailed through and the check reported a rollback failure that was really a test bug. Same trap as 'assert the state the code READS', third occurrence in this project."
    "DEFENSIVE OPTIONAL CHAINING IS NOT PARANOIA WHEN HARNESSES PASS FAKES. Reaching into io.sockets.adapter.rooms unguarded crashed two harnesses that pass a fake io. Guarded all the way down AND made to fail closed: if the room cannot be seen, the alert is not claimed as seen and stays queued."
  ]
}

@open_items_closed_2026_10_06 {
  @summary [
    "The four items review #5 left open are now fixed, each negative-controlled individually. Three of the four turned out to be wider than filed."
  ]

  @fixed [
    "AUTH REFUSAL IS RETRIED. socket.ts authenticateSocket(attempt) retries at 2s/5s/11s — past the 10s limiter window rather than into it — and sets userId from the ack. Previously a refusal was TERMINAL: the socket stayed connected so neither handleReconnect (driven by disconnect/connect_error) nor another connect ever fired, leaving a healthy-looking socket in no rooms with no state and no replay. THE SIBLING PATH: cancelAuthRetry is called from FOUR places, because connect() and disconnect() both removeAllListeners() BEFORE socket.disconnect(), so the disconnect HANDLER does not run on either — a timer surviving connect() would run a second retry chain against the new socket."
    "THE LIMITER SWEEP NO LONGER HANDS BACK BUDGETS. Two defects, one cause. lastUsed was written only by limitersFor, which runs once per CONNECTION, so the sweep's own safety argument was false for any actively-playing user. And the socket CAPTURED the limiter closures, which keep working after their map entry is swept, so a second tab built a fresh independent budget. Together every published limit became per-tab for tabs opened >60s apart — the multiplication S9 exists to prevent, reintroduced through its own memory sweep. Fixed by resolving per event: limitersFor(userId)[kind]()."
    "CASCADE-DELETED SERVERS RELEASE DRAINS FIRST. Backdoor.server is onDelete: Cascade and three paths delete servers (serverService, darknetDungeonService, adminApi/servers). After the delete no row remains to say whose resources the backdoor cost, so the consumer is unreachable by every release path — removeBackdoor truthfully answers 'No backdoor installed on this server' while the drain keeps charging. releaseDrainsForServers(serverIds) now runs before the delete at all three. The harness proves the ORDERING: release, assert gone, re-register, delete, then assert a post-delete call CANNOT free anything."
    "registerForumAccount ENFORCES DISCOVERY AND PROXY. It took a raw forumId from `forum register` and checked only existence. Discovery is now required (accessForum auto-discovers rather than refusing, so the legitimate route is unchanged — access then register; the error says so, because 'Forum not found' for a forum that plainly exists reads as a bug). The proxy requirement, which only accessForum enforced, now applies here too — checked against LIVE proxy status rather than accessForum's useProxy flag, deliberately NOT shared: one asks 'did this request opt in', the other 'is this player actually behind one', and collapsing them would change accessForum as a side effect."
  ]

  @lessons [
    "A CLEANUP HANDLER ON AN EVENT IS NOT A CLEANUP PATH. The client's `disconnect` listener cleared the auth retry timer and looked sufficient — but both programmatic teardowns call removeAllListeners() before socket.disconnect(), so the handler never runs on the paths that matter. When teardown strips listeners first, cleanup belongs at the CALL SITE, not in the event."
    "A MEMORY SWEEP CAN UNDO A CORRECTNESS INVARIANT. The limiter sweep was introduced to bound a map and quietly reintroduced per-socket budget multiplication, because deleting the entry does not stop the captured closures. Whenever something is evicted for memory reasons, ask what still holds a reference to it and what rebuilding it grants."
    "PROVE THE ORDERING, NOT JUST THE CALL. For a release-before-delete invariant, asserting the release works is half the test. The other half is showing the same call AFTER the delete cannot work — otherwise the ordering is an untested assumption and a future refactor will move it."
  ]
}
